const ANSWER_KEY = {"1": "A", "2": "A", "3": "D", "4": "B", "5": "C", "6": "A", "7": "C", "8": "C", "9": "D", "10": "C", "11": "A", "12": "C", "13": "C", "14": "B", "15": "E", "16": "E", "17": "A", "18": "A", "19": "B", "20": "C", "21": "E", "22": "C", "23": "E", "24": "C", "25": "A", "26": "C", "27": "D", "28": "C", "29": "B", "30": "C", "31": "E", "32": "D", "33": "E", "34": "A", "35": "A", "36": "A", "37": "A", "38": "B", "39": "E", "40": "E", "41": "A", "42": "A", "43": "C", "44": "B", "45": "D", "46": "C", "47": "D", "48": "B", "49": "F", "50": "C"};
const EXPLANATIONS = {"1": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Vulvovaginal candidiasis involves the overgrowth of normal vaginal Candida flora, most commonly Candida albicans. Symptoms include vulvar pruritus and erythema, burning, dyspareunia, and a thick, white vaginal discharge. A frequent risk factor is recent antibiotic use. Treatment includes intravaginal antifungal agents or oral fluconazole.</p><p>This patient’s presenting signs and symptoms of vaginal pruritus, burning, and dyspareunia after a recent course of antibiotics are suggestive of vulvovaginitis from Candida albicans. While Candida species compose an element of normal vaginal flora, overgrowth can occur and lead to vulvovaginitis. C. albicans is the most frequently associated species. Candida as a cause of vulvovaginitis can be distinguished from other potential causes by abnormal, often white, vaginal discharge with normal vaginal pH, negative KOH testing, and absence of motile organisms on wet mount preparation. Microscopy will show pseudohyphae, which can confirm the diagnosis. Risk factors for Candida vulvovaginitis include the use of medications, especially antibiotics and oral contraceptives, use of intravaginal or intrauterine contraceptive devices, and an immunocompromised state. Candida vulvovaginitis is treated with intravaginal antifungal agents such as clotrimazole, miconazole, or nystatin, or with oral fluconazole.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. Gardnerella vaginalis (Choice B) is the most common causative organism of bacterial vaginosis, which occurs as a result of a shift of vaginal flora, allowing its overgrowth. Bacterial vaginosis presents with gray, thin, malodorous discharge. Clue cells on wet mount preparation are diagnostic. It does not cause vulvar pruritus or burning. Neisseria gonorrhoeae (Choice D) is a sexually transmitted bacterium that classically presents with mucopurulent discharge and pruritus, although it may be asymptomatic. Complications include urethritis, cervicitis, pelvic inflammatory disease, Fitz-Hugh—Curtis syndrome, disseminated gonorrhea, and septic arthritis. It does not typically cause vaginal burning and would be less likely than vulvovaginal candidiasis to present immediately after a course of antibiotics. Trichomonas vaginalis (Choice E) is an anaerobic, flagellated protozoan that causes vaginal trichomoniasis, a sexually transmitted infection. It presents with pruritus, dysuria, dyspareunia, cervical erythema, and abnormal discharge that is described as green, frothy, and malodorous. While this patient does have pruritus and dyspareunia, she has no abnormal discharge, making trichomoniasis unlikely.</p>", "2": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: The first prenatal visit is used to establish a baseline history, evaluate maternal health, and screen for genetic disorders prevalent in that patient's population. Genetic screening in the United States for white patients routinely includes aneuploidies, cystic fibrosis, spinal muscular atrophy, and Tay-Sachs disease. Screening for additional disorders can be tailored to the patient’s family history and ethnicity.</p><p>The first prenatal visit is used to establish a baseline medical and gynecologic history, date the pregnancy, and perform laboratory and genetic screening for potential maternal or fetal health issues. Genetic screening can include screening for aneuploidies (eg, trisomy 21, 18, and 13 and sex chromosome aneuploidies) with cell-free DNA from maternal blood and genetic carrier screening for heritable disorders present in that population. In white patients, these disorders include cystic fibrosis, Tay-Sachs disease, and spinal muscular atrophy. Screening for additional disorders can be tailored based on the patient's ethnicity and the disorders found in that population (eg, Amish, Ashkenazi Jewish, African American). Cystic fibrosis is an autosomal recessive disorder characterized by failure to pass meconium in infancy, recurrent sinus and pulmonary infections, digital clubbing, bronchiectasis, and pancreatic insufficiency due to deficient trafficking of a membrane chloride channel. Cystic fibrosis carrier state increases the risk that the child may have the disorder if the father is either a carrier or affected. The first prenatal visit should also include routine laboratory studies (eg, complete blood count, basic metabolic panel) assessing risk for hemolytic disease of the newborn (eg, ABO, Rh(D) type, antibody screen), risk for acquiring communicable diseases (eg, rubella antibodies), risk for pyelonephritis (eg, urine culture to screen for asymptomatic bacteriuria), and risk for prenatal transmission of sexually transmitted infections (eg, screening for maternal Chlamydia trachomatis, Neisseria gonorrhoeae, HIV, syphilis, hepatitis B).</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. patient should be offered a 1-hour glucose tolerance test to screen for gestational diabetes at 24 to 28 weeks. Serum lipid studies (Choice C) are not routinely recommended during pregnancy and do not often change management, as statin therapy is contraindicated in pregnancy. Should this patient develop risk factors concerning for hyperlipidemia (eg, obesity, diabetes), a lipid panel can be discussed after her postpartum recovery is complete. Thalassemia screening (Choice D) is not routinely offered at the first prenatal visit for white patients unless risk factors are present, such as a history of recurrent pregnancy loss. If this patient's initial laboratory studies show microcytic anemia lower than the expected mild physiologic anemia of pregnancy (&lt;10.5 g/dL), screening for hemoglobinopathies including thalassemia should be offered. Chorionic villus sampling (Choice E) is an invasive test used to identify genetic abnormalities of the fetus. As it carries risk to the fetus, it is often reserved for further evaluation of abnormalities on aneuploidy screens or ultrasonography. There is no indication of a genetic abnormality in this patient, making chorionic villus sampling an inappropriate recommendation at this time.</p>", "3": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Antepartum intrauterine fetal demise commonly presents with decreased fetal movement and lack of fetal heart tones on examination. This often occurs as a result of chromosomal abnormalities, uteroplacental insufficiency, maternal medical disease or infection, and preeclampsia. The diagnosis is confirmed with ultrasonography, which will show the absence of fetal cardiac activity.</p><p>Antepartum intrauterine fetal demise commonly presents with decreased fetal movement and lack of fetal heart tones on examination. Ultrasonography confirms the absence of cardiac activity and intrauterine fetal demise. In high-income countries, stillbirth is most commonly the result of chromosomal abnormalities, uteroplacental insufficiency, and maternal medical disease, whereas in low-income countries it is more commonly the result of maternal infection, preeclampsia, or obstructed labor. Delivery for antepartum intrauterine fetal demise is based on the mother’s wishes, with immediate induction or delayed natural delivery both feasible options in the absence of any complicating medical issues. Labor typically begins 1 to 2 weeks after fetal demise. Coagulation abnormalities can occur with delayed delivery due to the release of tissue factor (thromboplastin) from the placenta. This can lead to disseminated intravascular coagulation, which presents with thrombocytopenia, prolongation of partial thromboplastin and prothrombin times, increased D-dimer concentrations, and decreased fibrinogen.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E. Fetal nonstress test (Choice A) is an antepartum test frequently performed for at-risk pregnancies. It measures the fetal heart rate and its response to fetal movements, which can be an important predictor of the health of the pregnancy. There are no fetal heart tones heard on this patient’s examination, which would make a fetal nonstress test ineffective. Kleihauer-Betke (acid elution) test (Choice B) is used to quantify the amount of fetal blood to which the maternal circulation has been exposed in order to determine adequacy of Rh(D) immune globulin dosing. It is frequently used when an Rh(D)-negative mother gives birth to an Rh(D)-positive child. While this patient may benefit from a Kleihauer-Betke test after delivery depending on her Rh(D) status, the test would play no role in confirming fetal demise. Measurement of maternal serum β-hCG concentration (Choice C) is used in the diagnosis of pregnancy and in the surveillance of choriocarcinoma. A slower than normal rate of increase in serum β-hCG concentration can also be seen in ectopic pregnancy. However, its concentration would be unlikely to change substantially shortly after fetal demise, which would prevent this test from confirming the diagnosis. Amniocentesis (Choice E) is an invasive sampling of amniotic fluid through the abdominal wall, usually performed at 15 to 20 weeks. It is useful for the diagnosis of fetal chromosomal abnormalities and of chorioamnionitis. As the risk for injury to the fetus increases with later gestational ages, amniocentesis at 25 weeks would be inappropriate. Additionally, it would not indicate whether a viable fetus remained.</p>", "4": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Diminished ovarian reserve is a common cause of infertility in pre- and perimenopausal patients. The decreased secretion of estradiol from the ovarian follicles leads to an increased concentration of follicle-stimulating hormone, especially early in the follicular phase.</p><p>Infertility may be caused by diminished ovarian reserve, anovulation, fallopian tube occlusion, sperm hypomotility or immotility, inability of sperm to penetrate the corona radiata of the ovum, structural abnormalities of the uterus, and thyroid disorders, among other causes. In this patient, given her age, the most likely cause of infertility is diminished ovarian reserve. As a patient ages, there is a depletion or dysfunction of ovarian follicles. In general, the first half of the menstrual cycle is referred to as the follicular phase, during which follicle-stimulating hormone and luteinizing hormone (FSH and LH, respectively) concentrations increase and stimulate the developing follicle. This follicle then produces estrogen, which leads to proliferation of the endometrium in preparation for implantation. An LH surge causes ovulation, after which the luteal phase begins, with the corpus luteum secreting progesterone to maintain the endometrial lining. If no implantation occurs, the corpus luteum degrades, estrogen and progesterone decrease, and menstruation occurs. In patients with diminished ovarian reserve, FSH will be increased early in the follicular phase (typically measured on day 3 of the menstrual cycle), as more hormone is needed to stimulate the remaining follicles, and there is less negative feedback on FSH from the existing follicles.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E. estradiol concentration is less able to adequately suppress FSH release, resulting in an increased FSH concentration early in the follicular phase. Luteinizing hormone (Choice C) may increase during menopause, when serum estradiol concentrations decrease and there is less negative feedback on the release of LH. During normal menstruation, LH stimulates ovulation and promotes progesterone production from the corpus luteum. This patient likely has normal concentrations of LH early in her follicular phase as she continues to have normal ovulation and menstrual periods. Prolactin (Choice D) is unlikely to be increased in this patient. High concentrations of prolactin can inhibit gonadotropin-releasing hormone as well as FSH and LH. It most commonly presents with galactorrhea but may also cause amenorrhea. This patient has had no recent nipple discharge, making hyperprolactinemia unlikely. Thyroid-stimulating hormone (Choice E) is increased in hypothyroidism, which most frequently presents with fatigue, weight gain, constipation, cold intolerance, and oligomenorrhea. It may also cause infertility as a result of anovulation. This patient has no other signs of hypothyroidism, such as weight gain, making a depleted ovarian reserve the most likely cause of her infertility.</p>", "5": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: A diagnosis of major depressive disorder is made when a patient experiences five or more of the following symptoms during a 2-week period or greater: depressed mood, anhedonia, change in appetite or weight, insomnia or hypersomnia, psychomotor agitation or retardation, fatigue, feelings of worthlessness, diminished ability to think or concentrate, and recurrent thoughts of death including suicidal ideation with or without a plan. Postpartum unipolar major depression occurs prior to or within 12 months after giving birth.</p><p>This patient's presentation meets criteria for major depressive disorder (MDD) with peripartum onset, which presents with the typical symptoms of MDD (five or more of the following during a 2-week period or greater): depressed mood, decreased energy, anhedonia, sleep disturbances, changes in appetite or weight, guilt, feelings of worthlessness, poor concentration, psychomotor retardation, and recurrent thoughts of death (which can include suicidal ideation) and may be delayed by as much as 12 months after delivery. Risk factors for MDD with peripartum onset include sleep disturbance, childcare stress, decreased estrogen concentrations, breast-feeding difficulty, body image dissatisfaction, and poor postpartum social support. Consequences of MDD with peripartum onset can be severe and include impaired bonding with the child, developmental delays in the child, child neglect, marital discord, suicide, and infanticide. The relative prevalence and severe consequences of this diagnosis necessitate proactive screening for depression in primary care settings. Patients who screen positive must be assessed for suicidal or homicidal ideation. Treatment of moderate to severe MDD with peripartum onset typically includes cognitive behavioral therapy in combination with selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors, which pose minimal risk to breast-feeding infants, and inpatient admission in high-risk situations.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, E, and F. Bipolar disorder (Choice A) is characterized by discrete episodes of depression and hypomania or mania, which can sometimes be accompanied by psychotic symptoms such as hallucinations and delusions. This patient does not demonstrate mania currently nor has a history of past manic episodes. Generalized anxiety disorder (Choice B) requires the presence of excessive worry about numerous aspects of daily life occurring on most days for at least 6 months. Patients with this diagnosis have a consequent inability to control symptoms of anxiety, and have at least three other symptoms (irritability, muscle tension, sleep problems, poor concentration, fatigue, restlessness). These symptoms are absent in this patient. Postpartum psychosis (Choice D) refers to the postpartum onset of psychotic symptoms such as delusions, hallucinations, and/or disorganized thinking or behavior. This patient has not experienced hallucinations and illustrates a linear thought process devoid of disorganization or delusions when answering the physician's questions about her symptoms. Schizoaffective disorder (Choice E) is characterized by psychotic symptoms (delusions, hallucinations, disorganized speech, disorganized behavior, and/or negative symptoms) along with significant mood episodes (major depressive or manic episodes). The total duration of symptoms must exceed 6 months. This patient does not demonstrate psychotic symptoms and does not meet the duration criteria of schizoaffective disorder. Normal grief reaction (Choice F), or bereavement, refers to a person's emotional response after a loved one dies. This can include sadness, shock, anguish, guilt, regret, difficulty concentrating, decreased interest in usual activities, preoccupation with thoughts and memories of the deceased, and neurovegetative symptoms such as sleep, appetite, and energy disturbances. This patient did not experience the death of a loved one.</p>", "6": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Ectopic pregnancy is a nonviable pregnancy that implants outside of the uterus, most commonly in the fallopian tube. Symptoms include abdominal pain and vaginal bleeding in the presence of a positive pregnancy test. Ultrasonography can be used to aid in diagnosis. Treatment includes methotrexate for small, early pregnancies and laparoscopy for larger or complicated pregnancies.</p><p>Ectopic pregnancy is an abnormal pregnancy in which the fertilized ovum implants in the fallopian tube (most common), on the ovary, within the peritoneal cavity, or in any nonendometrial location. It often presents with abdominal pain and vaginal bleeding in a patient with a missed or delayed period and positive pregnancy test, as seen in this patient. Ultrasonography can be used to further delineate between an intrauterine pregnancy and may show an ectopic pregnancy depending on its size. An ectopic pregnancy is not viable. Treatment can occur with methotrexate for small, early pregnancies, but may require salpingectomy or evacuation with laparoscopy for larger pregnancies or in cases of medical treatment failure. Complications of ectopic pregnancy include rupture, bleeding, and hemoperitoneum. This patient has a large, ill- defined mass in her right lower quadrant associated with abdominal pain and a positive pregnancy test, making an ectopic pregnancy most likely. Given the large size of the mass, the most appropriate next step in management would be admission to the hospital for laparoscopy and removal of the ectopic pregnancy to prevent further complications.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, E, and F. v postoperative pain and complications. While a laparotomy is occasionally necessary during a laparoscopic approach to allow access to the operative site, laparoscopy is usually sufficient for treating an ectopic pregnancy. CT scan of the pelvis (Choice C) and MRI of pelvis (Choice E) are not necessary and would delay the appropriate treatment of this patient. While both imaging modalities may offer better resolution of the ill-defined mass seen on ultrasonography, neither would change the treatment strategy. Even if there were concerns for another right lower quadrant pathology, such as appendicitis, treatment in this scenario would still be with laparoscopy. Delaying management to obtain further imaging also increases the risk for rupture and its associated complications. Limited-stay observation (Choice D) is inappropriate. This patient is likely experiencing an ectopic pregnancy based on her abdominal pain, positive pregnancy test, and ill- defined right lower quadrant mass. Delaying treatment with laparoscopy increases the risk for rupture, bleeding, and consequent hemodynamic instability. She should be treated urgently, rather than remain in observation. Uterine evacuation in the operating room (Choice F) would be ineffective in treating this patient's pain and the underlying cause of her presentation. Ectopic pregnancies are, by definition, implanted in a space outside of the uterine cavity. Most commonly this is the fallopian tube. Therefore, the more appropriate treatment for this patient would be laparoscopy, rather than uterine evacuation.</p>", "7": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Amenorrhea may be caused by a variety of etiologies, including menopause and pregnancy. Menopause refers to the cessation of menses due to the depletion of ovarian follicles and is often associated with hot flashes, night sweats, mood changes, sleep disturbances, and vaginal atrophy. When these symptoms occur in patients who are younger than age 45 years, measurement of serum β-hCG concentration should be completed to rule out pregnancy as the underlying cause of their symptoms.</p><p>Amenorrhea may be caused by pregnancy, menopause or premature ovarian failure, abnormalities or immaturity of the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis, inadequate nutrition, chromosomal abnormalities, and thyroid disorders. As women age, their ovarian reserve is depleted and they have fewer ovulatory cycles until menstruation stops completely, a phase of life referred to as menopause. Menopause is characterized by the lack of ovarian estrogen production, resulting in manifestations such as vaginal atrophy, hot flashes, and night sweats. Sleep disturbances and mood changes may also be present. While menopause has a median age of onset of about 50 years old, symptoms can begin much earlier than that in some patients. Menopause before age 45 years is referred to as early menopause, whereas menopause occurring before age 40 years is often caused by primary ovarian insufficiency. Younger patients who present with signs of menopause such as irregular menses should first be evaluated with measurement of serum β-hCG concentration to rule out pregnancy as the underlying cause of their symptoms. This is especially pertinent for this patient with a slightly enlarged uterus, which may be caused by pregnancy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. . Measurement of serum estradiol concentration (Choice A) is not required in this patient. Estradiol is typically increased during pregnancy but decreased during menopause. However, because the estrogen concentration varies based on the phase of the menstrual cycle, estradiol measurements are often difficult to interpret in isolation. Thus, this is not the best next step in diagnosis for this patient. Measurement of serum follicle-stimulating hormone concentration (Choice B) is increased in menopause but decreased in pregnancy. While it can be used as a confirmatory test for menopause, this is more commonly diagnosed clinically. While this patient has many symptoms of menopause, it is important to first rule out pregnancy as a cause of her symptoms given that she is of reproductive age. Measurement of serum thyroid-stimulating hormone concentration (Choice D) could be used to evaluate the patient for hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism. Hypothyroidism presents with fatigue, weight gain, constipation, cold intolerance, oligomenorrhea, bradycardia, dry and edematous skin, and delayed relaxation of deep tendon reflexes. In contrast, hyperthyroidism presents with heat intolerance, weight loss, palpitations, diaphoresis, and diarrhea. While both may cause oligomenorrhea or amenorrhea, this patient has no other signs or symptoms to suggest one of these diseases. Progestin challenge test (Choice E) is used in the evaluation of amenorrhea. A short course of progesterone is prescribed, and the patient is observed for uterine bleeding after its withdrawal. If bleeding occurs, the patient has adequate concentrations of estrogen to create an endometrial lining; if it does not, then the patient likely has hypoestrogenism. It is important to determine if this patient is pregnant prior to performing a progestin challenge test as the test could negatively affect an existing pregnancy.</p>", "8": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Heavy or irregular menstrual bleeding in adolescent patients is often secondary to anovulation in the setting of an immature hypothalamic-pituitary- ovarian axis. This immaturity causes irregular and noncyclic gonadotropin-releasing hormone release from the hypothalamus. Anovulation presents with abnormal intervals of menstruation, associated with heavy vaginal bleeding when menses occur, as a result of continued endometrial development secondary to unopposed estrogen production. Oral contraceptives are used as a first-line therapy to normalize the menstrual cycle and stabilize the endometrial lining.</p><p>Anovulation presents with irregular intervals of menstruation, associated with heavy vaginal bleeding when menses occur, as a result of continued endometrial development secondary to unopposed estrogen production. Common causes of anovulation include abnormalities or immaturity of the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis, pregnancy, polycystic ovary syndrome, menopause or premature ovarian failure, inadequate nutrition, and chromosomal abnormalities. This patient’s symptoms are most likely secondary to immaturity of the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis, given her young age. Normal development of the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis takes time, with immaturity resulting in irregular and noncyclic gonadotropin-releasing hormone release from the hypothalamus. This causes varying concentrations of follicle- stimulating hormone (FSH) and luteinizing hormone (LH), leading to anovulation and amenorrhea. This also affects the concentrations of estrogen and progesterone in the body, resulting in varying degrees of secondary sexual characteristic progression. Oral contraceptive therapy can be used to normalize the menstrual cycle and decrease the amount of menstrual bleeding by stabilizing the endometrium.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. Clomiphene therapy (Choice A) is a medication commonly used to treat infertility caused by anovulation. It acts as an estrogen receptor antagonist in the hypothalamus. preventing negative feedback on gonadotropin-releasing hormone, which leads to increased concentrations of FSH and LH, resulting in increased ovulation. It would be less likely than oral contraceptive therapy to normalize this patient's menstrual cycle or decrease her bleeding. Leuprolide therapy (Choice B) is a gonadotropin-releasing hormone agonist that is useful for the treatment of heavy or painful menstrual bleeding, such as that seen in menorrhagia or endometriosis. However, it is typically only used after a trial of oral contraceptive therapy, as it is an injectable medication. Dilatation and curettage (Choice D) is useful in treating heavy or abnormal uterine bleeding as it removes the endometrial lining from the uterus. However, it is an invasive surgical option and should be reserved for patients with hemodynamic instability or for patients who do not respond to less invasive options, such as oral contraceptive therapy. No treatment indicated (Choice E) is incorrect. This patient has heavy, ongoing menstrual bleeding associated with anemia. Failing to normalize her menstrual cycle and stabilize her endometrial lining with oral contraceptive therapy would likely lead to ongoing or recurrent bleeding with even further decreases in her already low hemoglobin concentration.</p>", "9": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Variable decelerations are defined as an abrupt decrease in heart rate of at least 15 beats per minute that last at least 15 seconds but less than 2 minutes. They are unrelated to the timing of uterine contractions. Their occurrence indicates umbilical cord compression, which may be managed expectantly when other concerning features are absent, or with maternal repositioning and amnioinfusion.</p><p>A healthy fetal heart rate is between 110 and 160 beats per minute (bpm) with 6 to 25 bpm of variability and spontaneous accelerations. Variable decelerations, as well as intermittent early decelerations, can also occur during the normal process of labor. Variable decelerations are defined as an abrupt decrease in heart rate of atleast 15 bpm that last at least 15 seconds, but less than 2 minutes. They do not show a specific relation to the uterine contraction tracing. They are caused by umbilical cord compression that results in decreased blood flow to the fetus, an increased peripheral systemic vascular resistance, and an autonomic response of fetal bradycardia. These decelerations can cause fetal hypoxemia and acidemia if prolonged, frequent, or paired with other concerning signs of fetal distress, such as late decelerations, absent variability, or minimal accelerations. Treatment can include maternal repositioning and amnioinfusion to alleviate the compression by repleting the volume of fluid in the amniotic cavity.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E. Cervical dilation (Choice A) progresses from a closed os at the start of labor to 10 cm dilated at the end of the first stage of labor. During this time, the cervix also becomes effaced, and the fetal head becomes engaged in the pelvis. Cervical dilation does not cause a particular fetal heart pattern. Fetal head compression (Choice B) can cause early decelerations, defined as a gradual decrease in the heart rate that nadirs more than 30 seconds after the initiation of heart rate decline. The nadir of heart rate occurs at the same time as the peak of the uterine contraction. This fetal heart rate tracing shows abrupt decelerations that are unrelated to the uterine contractions, making variable decelerations secondary to umbilical cord compression the most likely cause. Fetal movement (Choice C) occurs naturally during pregnancy. As the pregnancy reaches term, fetal movement often decreases as the fetus enlarges in size relative to the amniotic sac and uterus. Fetal movement is often associated with fetal heart rate accelerations, rather than the decelerations shown on this fetal heart tracing. Uteroplacental insufficiency (Choice E) can be caused by hypertension, diabetes, cocaine or tobacco use, coagulopathies, prolonged uterine contraction, uterine infection, late-term pregnancy, or maternal hypotension or hypoxia. It causes late decelerations in the fetal heart rate tracing, defined as gradual decreases in the fetal heart rate that begins after the peak of uterine contraction. The decelerations in this case are unrelated in timing to the uterine contractions, which makes uteroplacental insufficiency unlikely despite this patient's history of tobacco use.</p>", "10": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Breast cysts present commonly in premenopausal women with a large, rubbery, tender mass with no accompanying lymphadenopathy, overlying skin changes, or nipple discharge. Ultrasonography will show a cystic lesion. Treatment includes fine-needle aspiration.</p><p>This premenopausal patient with a tender cystic lesion likely has a breast cyst. Breast cysts are benign lesions that may be found in isolation or as part of fibrocystic changes. They may be hormonally responsive and fluctuate in size during menses. However, thorough evaluation is necessary for any patient who presents with a breast mass as this can be the first presenting sign of breast cancer, the most common nondermatologic cancer in women. Evaluation should include a thorough history, including family history, and physical examination. Physical examination typically shows a smooth, rubbery, freely mobile mass with no coexisting skin changes, lymphadenopathy, or nipple discharge in benign lesions. In contrast, malignant lesions often present with irregular, firm, adherent masses. Further evaluation includes diagnostic imaging with either mammography or ultrasonography. If ultrasonography shows a cystic lesion, fine-needle aspiration should be completed to see if the mass resolves and to treat the patient’s associated symptoms. If the cyst disappears and if fluid analysis is benign, no further treatment is warranted. If it does not, biopsy should be performed to rule out malignancy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. Second breast examination in 6 months (Choice A) is incorrect. Any patient who presents with a breast mass should be evaluated to rule out malignancy. Mammography (Choice B) would not be of benefit for this patient. Ultrasonography is usually used in the diagnosis of breast lesions in women under age 35 years, as their breast tissue is often too dense to be adequately assessed by mammography. Ultrasonography was able to evaluate this lesion, and the next most appropriate step in management is fine-needle aspiration. True-cut needle biopsy (Choice D) would not be useful in managing this patient’s lesion. True-cut needle biopsy takes a core biopsy of a mass, which is more beneficial in the diagnosis of solid, rather than cystic, lesions. Fine-needle aspiration would be more helpful in managing this patient's symptoms and confirming the diagnosis. Lumpectomy (Choice E) is used frequently in the setting of breast masses that are concerning for malignancy. A lumpectomy is a surgical excision of a breast lump, which is then sent for pathologic evaluation. It is more invasive than fine-needle aspiration, which may cause resolution of this patient's mass.</p>", "11": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Patients who possess decisional capacity have the right to decline procedures, even if the procedure is emergent and/or involves a fetus. The ethical principle of autonomy should be honored in these situations.</p><p>According to the ethical tenet of autonomy, this patient has the right to refuse labor induction despite the risks to herself and her fetus. Therefore, the most appropriate course of action is to await spontaneous labor because of her refusal. Patients are assumed to possess decisional capacity unless they demonstrate otherwise. Despite her generalized seizure, she remains alert, suggesting that she continues to have decisional capacity. Patients who possess decisional capacity have the ethical right to decline procedures, even if the procedures are emergent and/or involve a fetus. For patients who lack decisional capacity or are unable to communicate a decision about an emergent procedure, the ethical principle of beneficence should prevail in that the physician should save the patient's life. This patient has eclampsia, a life-threatening complication of pregnancy, and a fetus that is unlikely to survive after delivery given its severe, underlying genetic abnormalities. Despite this, the patient's wishes to avoid induction of labor should be honored.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. = avoid delivering a preterm infant. However, her wishes should still be followed even if her clinical situation worsens provided that she demonstrates a clear and consistent choice, with awareness of the risks. Deliver the fetus because her husband will provide consent on her behalf (Choice C) is unethical. This patient has decisional capacity, and she retains autonomy over her body. Even though her husband disagrees with her decision, the ethical tenet of autonomy allows this patient to continue to refuse induction of labor despite her husband's wishes. Deliver the fetus because the patient's life is threatened (Choice D) is incorrect. The patient's life is threatened with eclampsia, which is only relieved by delivery of the fetus. However, if these risks have been explained to her and she still declines induction, these wishes should be honored. Deliver the fetus because trisomy 18 is a terminal condition (Choice E) is not appropriate. Trisomy 18 is a terminal condition that results in severe congenital defects and abnormalities, with most patients dying by the age of 6 months. However, regardless of the condition of the fetus, the induction of labor must still be consented to by the mother.</p>", "12": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Cervical insufficiency is the inability of the cervix to contain the pregnancy (the fetus, placenta, and amniotic sac) without the presence of contractions, labor, or both, typically during the second or early third trimester. Risk factors include prior cervical trauma due to previous delivery or surgical manipulation. It is managed with cervical cerclage.</p><p>The American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists defines cervical insufficiency as the inability of the cervix to contain the pregnancy (the fetus, placenta, and amniotic sac) without the presence of contractions, labor, or both, typically during the second or early third trimester. The condition is associated with miscarriage, preterm or prelabor rupture of membranes, and preterm birth. Risk factors include cervical trauma from prior delivery or surgical manipulation, such as a cone biopsy, as seen in this patient. Classically, when symptomatic, it presents with feelings of fullness in the lower pelvis in the absence of contractions or labor, similar to this patient's last pregnancy. Speculum examination may show a dilated or effaced cervix, or protrusion of the pregnancy through the cervical os into the vagina. Mild cases without dilation of the external os may go undetected. Cervical length measurements may show a shortened cervix, with a cervical length less than 15 to 25 mm. Treatment is indicated if the degree of cervical insufficiency is significant enough to threaten the pregnancy and in cases of previous cervical insufficiency. This is accomplished through cervical cerclage, which is the placement of a suture into the cervical os to narrow the canal and maintain a closed cervix. The suture is then removed at the time of labor, or around 36 to 38 weeks.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. abdominal trauma, smoking, or cocaine use, none of which are found in this patient. Prior preterm delivery is not a risk factor for abruptio placentae. Cervical cancer (Choice B) classically presents with vaginal bleeding, with or without intercourse. Risk factors for its development include human papillomavirus high risk strains 16, 18, 31, or 33 infections, early age at onset of sexual activity, tobacco use, oral contraceptive use, immunosuppression, and infection with HIV or other sexually transmitted infections. This patient has a prior history of cervical cancer that was treated appropriately with cone biopsy, making recurrence less likely than cervical insufficiency. Cervical stenosis (Choice D) is induced by any inflammatory, scarring process and presents with dysmenorrhea or cyclical pelvic pain associated with light flow during menses. If it involves the uterine cavity, it may also cause amenorrhea, infertility, and recurrent pregnancy loss. Given this patient's previous pregnancy loss with associated pelvic pressure but lack of uterine contractions, as well as her history of cone biopsy, cervical insufficiency is more likely than cervical stenosis. Placenta previa (Choice E) presents with painless vaginal bleeding during the third trimester due to a placenta that partially or completely overlies the cervical os. Risk factors for its development include causes of uterine scarring, such as multiparity, previous cesarean delivery, and prior dilatation and curettage. It often necessitates cesarean delivery to prevent fetomaternal hemorrhage. This patient has a low-lying placenta but no evidence of placenta previa on ultrasonography.</p>", "13": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: A cervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN) 3 is a high-grade cervical lesion caused most by human papillomavirus high-risk strains 16, 18, 31, or 33. It portends a high risk for progression to cervical carcinoma. If the entire squamocolumnar junction is visible, appropriate management includes curative ablation or excision. However, if it is not visible, cone biopsy of the cervix to evaluate the extent of the transformation zone is recommended to rule out carcinoma.</p><p>Acervical intraepithelial neoplasia (CIN) 3 is a high-grade cervical lesion with an increased risk for progression to cervical cancer. Cervical carcinoma is classically associated with infection from human papillomavirus (HPV) high-risk strains 16, 18, 31, and 33. Risk factors include multiple sexual partners, smoking, and immunosuppression. Screening Pap smears are recommended every 3 years in women age 21 to 29 years and every 5 years in conjunction with HPV cotesting (every 3 years if cotesting is not performed) in women age 30 to 65 years. Abnormal Pap smear cytology results are evaluated by colposcopy, which allows for the visual assessment and biopsy of abnormal lesions. A colposcopy is deemed inadequate if the entirety of the squamocolumnar junction or transformation zone is not visible, as there is a possibility of undiagnosed carcinoma. In this patient's case, the biopsy confirms CIN 3 disease, which requires excision via either a cone biopsy of the cervix or a loop electrosurgical excision procedure (LEEP). The cone biopsy serves to further evaluate a larger region of the cervix for cervical carcinoma and may be curative if a margin of normal cervical tissue is identified on histology. Pap smear every 6 months (Choice A) would delay definitive evaluation and treatment of this patient's CIN 3. Her colposcopy did not visualize the entire transformation zone, and she requires excision biopsy with either cone biopsy of the cervix or LEEP to fully evaluate for cervical cancer. Delayed cervical cancer diagnosis and treatment is associated with significant morbidity and mortality. Hysteroscopy (Choice B) would allow visualization of the uterine cavity and is useful in diagnosing endometrial polyps or performing endometrial biopsies. It does not typically play a role in the diagnosis or management of cervical carcinoma. Endometrial biopsy (Choice D) is used to diagnose endometrial carcinoma, which commonly presents with postmenopausal vaginal bleeding or abnormal uterine bleeding in a premenopausal woman. If atypical glandular cells are noted on cervical cytology via Pap smear, endometrial biopsy is recommended to rule out endometrial carcinoma, especially in patients over age 35 years. This patient did not have glandular cells on cytology. Intravaginal radiation therapy (Choice E) is not the appropriate next step in management. Intravaginal radiation therapy may be used to treat locally advanced cervical cancer, vaginal cancer, and endometrial cancer. However, this patient has not yet been diagnosed with cervical cancer. She should first undergo diagnosis with cone biopsy of the cervix or LEEP prior to receiving treatment. Hysterectomy (Choice F) is a potential treatment option for recurrent CIN 2 or 3, as well as cervical carcinoma. However, it is important initially to fully evaluate the entirety of the cervical transformation zone via cone biopsy of the cervix prior to proceeding with hysterectomy.</p>", "14": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: A normal intrauterine pregnancy often presents with amenorrhea, nausea, breast tenderness, fatigue, and, occasionally, vaginal bleeding. Formal diagnosis is made with urine or serum β-hCG, and thus its measurement is the most appropriate next step for a patient of reproductive age with abnormal vaginal bleeding. Ultrasonography can be used to evaluate for an intrauterine gestational sac, which should be present by 5 weeks of gestation.</p><p>Measurement of serum β-hCG concentration is the most appropriate next step for a patient of reproductive age who presents with abnormal vaginal bleeding. Pregnancy is suspected when there is a missed or delayed menstrual period. Light vaginal bleeding can occur early in pregnancy, often due to implantation of the embryo, which can occasionally be mistaken for menses. Other symptoms of early pregnancy include nausea, breast tenderness, and fatigue. Physical examination may show an enlarged globular uterus, but formal diagnosis is made with urine or serum β-hCG. β-hCG is produced by the placenta, is undetectable in nonpregnant patients, and doubles approximately every 48 hours during an early normal pregnancy. In a normal pregnancy, an intrauterine gestational sac should be visible by 5 weeks of gestation. Additionally, transvaginal ultrasonography provides sufficient sensitivity to detect an intrauterine pregnancy once the β-hCG concentration exceeds 1000 to 2000 mIU/mL.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E. Measurement of serum follicle-stimulating hormone concentration (Choice A) can be used to evaluate for menopause and premature ovarian insufficiency, as it will be increased in these syndromes. They most often present with irregular menstruation or amenorrhea, hot flashes, and night sweats caused by anovulation and a low estrogen state. This patient has had regular menstruation up until the most recent cycle, and thus pregnancy is the more likely diagnosis. Progesterone suppositories (Choice C) are not of benefit for this patient at this time. The corpus luteum secretes progesterone after ovulation that maintains the endometrial lining and prepares it for embryo implantation, as well as decreases uterine contractility to support the developing embryo. Thus, it is most commonly used prophylactically to prevent recurrent early pregnancy loss or preterm labor for those at increased risk. Hysteroscopy (Choice D) is used to evaluate the uterus for structural causes of irregular menses, as it allows a complete visualization of the endometrium. However, this patient has previously had regular menses, making a structural abnormality unlikely. Additionally, hysteroscopy should not be done until pregnancy is ruled out. Dilatation and curettage (Choice E) is used to treat heavy or abnormal uterine bleeding that does not respond to less invasive interventions, as well as for removing fetal tissue or retained products of conception. Prior to pursuing dilatation and curettage, pregnancy should be confirmed or ruled out with serum β-hCG concentration.</p>", "15": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Stress urinary incontinence presents with the leakage of urine with increased intra-abdominal pressure, most commonly in multigravid women due to weakness of the pelvic floor muscles and the subsequent loss of proximal support of the urethra. This causes inferior displacement of the vesicourethral junction and hypermobility of the urethra with abdominal pressure. Treatment includes pelvic floor exercises, weight loss, pessary device placement, or surgical correction.</p><p>Stress urinary incontinence is common in multiparous women and in men following prostate surgery due to weakness of the muscles and connective tissues of the pelvic floor. In multiparous women, this is typically secondary to pregnancy with resultant mechanical stress on the vaginal wall and pelvic floor. This weakness of the pelvic floor in turn compromises the support of pelvic structures, including the uterus, bladder, urethra, and rectum. Increased abdominal pressure (eg, coughing, sneezing, laughing, heavy lifting) causes the vesicourethral junction to move out of its normal position, preventing sphincter closure, and resulting in the subsequent leakage of urine. It is commonly associated with urethral hypermobility, as evidenced by deviation greater than 30 degrees from the horizontal of an inserted cotton-tipped applicator with straining. It is often associated with a urethrocele, where the urethra prolapses into the anterior vaginal wall, as found in this patient, or out of the urethral opening itself due to the loss of its proximal support. Stress incontinence can also be associated with a cystocele, which also presents as a bulge on the anterior vaginal wall, but urethral hypermobility is typically absent. Regardless of the cause, the treatment of stress incontinence includes pelvic floor exercises and weight loss, with the potential need for pessary device placement or surgical correction if there is persistent incontinence. x Adverse effect of amitriptyline (Choice A) can include overflow urinary incontinence secondary to urinary retention due to the strong anticholinergic effects of tricyclic antidepressants. Overflow incontinence occurs when the intravesical hydrostatic pressure eventually overwhelms the internal and external urinary sphincter closing pressures, causing passive leakage of urine, not leakage with mechanical stress. Detrusor overactivity (Choice B) or hyperreflexia leads to urge incontinence due to uninhibited bladder contractions. It typically presents with the urge to urinate followed by the leakage of a variable amount of urine. It is most commonly associated with neurologic disorders (eg, multiple sclerosis, spinal stenosis, or spinal cord injuries) but may also be idiopathic. Estrogen deficiency (Choice C) in the setting of menopause can cause atrophy and inflammation of the vagina and urethra, leading to an overactive bladder (urgency, frequency, and urge incontinence). Pelvic examination typically shows vaginal atrophy and tenderness without other abnormalities. While this patient does show signs of estrogen deficiency (decreased rugation of the vaginal mucosa), leakage of urine with mechanical stress and prolapse of structures into the anterior vaginal wall is more consistent with stress incontinence. Impaired detrusor compliance (Choice D), which causes high bladder filling pressures, is a far less common cause of stress incontinence than loss of support of pelvic structures. Causes include spinal cord injury and radiation fibrosis. Intrinsic sphincter deficiency (Choice F) is another common cause of stress incontinence, with similar risk factors of multiple vaginal deliveries, obesity, and prostate surgery. This patient with examination findings consistent with a urethrocele or cystocele more likely has stress incontinence due to loss of urethral support.</p>", "16": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Oral contraceptives are an effective form of birth control. A pelvic or speculum examination is not required prior to their prescription.</p><p>Many methods of contraception exist, including temporary and permanent options, hormonal and nonhormonal devices, oral medications, injections, implants, and ovulation tracking. The most appropriate contraceptive method is dependent on the patient and their goals of care. Counseling regarding contraceptive options should determine if the patient wants long-term or temporary contraception, whether they are able to be compliant with medication every day, menstrual history, and any comorbidities that may make some options contraindicated. Oral contraceptives (OCPs) are a temporary form of birth control that must be taken every day. They commonly include combinations of ethinyl estradiol and progestin, although progestin-only options are available. OCPs work to prevent pregnancy by thickening the cervical mucus, thinning the endometrial lining, and decreasing ovulation. They are an effective method of contraception when taken as prescribed. A speculum and pelvic examination are not required prior to prescribing OCPs; however, they should be started after a patient turns 21 years old in accordance with the United States Preventive Services Task Force.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A RB C andn a patient should undergo a pelvic examination in the future to screen for cervical cancer, it is not necessary to complete the examination prior to prescribing oral contraception. Recommend the patient return in 6 months (Choice B) is inappropriate. Failing to prescribe this patient an OCP because she declined a pelvic examination puts her at risk for unintended pregnancy, with its associated social, emotional, and physical effects. Schedule an examination with a pediatric gynecologist (Choice C) is not necessary to prescribe this patient oral contraception. She is an adult at 19 years old and would be unlikely to consent to a speculum or pelvic examination in a different physician's office. Do not prescribe contraception until pelvic examination (Choice D) is incorrect. A pelvic or speculum examination is not recommended prior to prescribing oral contraception in an otherwise healthy patient. Failing to prescribe an adequate form of contraception may damage the patient-provider relationship.</p>", "17": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Arterial thromboembolism is the most common cause of acute limb ischemia, typically from a cardiogenic source. Noncardiogenic embolic disease may originate from abnormalities of the upper extremity arterial supply or from paradoxical emboli that cross from the venous to systemic circulations via an intracardiac or intrapulmonary shunt. Echocardiography is indicated for the evaluation of intracardiac shunts, which are suggested by characteristic auscultative findings such as abnormalities of S2.</p><p>This patient has signs and symptoms of acute limb ischemia, which is characterized by pain, pallor, poikilothermia, pulselessness, paresthesia, and paralysis. Arterial thromboembolism is a common cause of acute upper extremity limb ischemia, typically from a cardiogenic source such as atrial fibrillation, valvular heart disease, left ventricular aneurysm or hypokinesis, left atrial myxoma, or infective endocarditis. In this patient, the presence of venous thrombi raises concern for an intracardiac or intrapulmonary shunt that has allowed passage of a paradoxical embolism from the venous circulation to the systemic circulation, bypassing the lungs. Paradoxical emboli may traverse through a patent foramen ovale, atrial septal defect (ASD), ventricular septal defect, or pulmonary arteriovenous malformation. A split, fixed S2 suggests an ASD in this patient. Patients are at risk for stroke, acute limb ischemia, and thromboembolic injury to organs (eg, mesenteric ischemia). Transesophageal echocardiography is the best test to confirm the diagnosis. A bubble study, in which agitated saline is introduced to the venous system and timed until it is visualized in the left side of the heart, can help distinguish between intracardiac and intrapulmonary shunting. v Cardiac catheterization (Choice B) is indicated for the diagnosis and treatment of myocardial ischemia, which is suspected in ischemic changes on ECG (eg, ST depression or elevation) and/or echocardiography (eg, wall hypokinesis or valve rupture). While cardiac catheterization can disclose findings associated with intracardiac shunt (eg, higher than expected right-sided oxygen saturation), echocardiography is more sensitive and specific. Placement of an inferior vena cava filter (Choice C) is used to prevent the embolization of deep venous thrombosis to the pulmonary vasculature in patients who have a contraindication to anticoagulation. This patient does not have a contraindication to anticoagulation. Thrombectomy of the left lower extremity (Choice D) venous thrombi is reserved for patients who fail therapeutic anticoagulation or are suffering phlegmasia cerulea dolens (due to high risk for developing compartment syndrome). Were this patient to fail first-line treatment of her venous thrombi, thrombectomy should be approached with extreme caution, as it can cause downstream showering of emboli. This could in turn lead to further arterial limb ischemia, mesenteric ischemia, or stroke in patients with intracardiac or intrapulmonary shunt. Tubal ligation (Choice E) is a permanent contraceptive option that can be offered to patients who have completed childbearing or who do not desire to ever bear children. While the patient's postpartum state puts her at higher risk for venous thrombosis and subsequent paradoxical embolism, offering prevention of further pregnancy at this time will not provide adequate workup or treatment of her acute limb ischemia.</p>", "18": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: HIV testing is recommended at least once during pregnancy with repeat testing recommended in the third trimester for those at high risk for infection. While transmission can occur during any point in the peripartum period, it most commonly occurs during delivery or breast-feeding. However, this risk can be minimized by providing the mother with appropriate antiretroviral therapy. If a patient declines an HIV test, the reasons should be explored with an aim to form a therapeutic relationship that will enable testing in the future.</p><p>The United States Preventive Services Task Force and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention recommend that everyone between the teenage years and age 65 be tested for HIV at least once. Those at higher risk should be screened more frequently. Additionally, as HIV may be transmitted from a mother to a child during pregnancy, delivery, and breast-feeding, screening for the infection is also recommended at least once during pregnancy. If the initial test is negative, a repeat test is recommended in the third trimester for those at high risk for infection. Transmission to the fetus is uncommon if the mother is taking antiretroviral therapy and has a low HIV viral load. This patient is declining HIV testing, despite being at high risk. Her healthcare provider should evaluate her reasons for declining the test and continue to provide support for her developing pregnancy. After forming a therapeutic alliance with the patient, she may be more responsive to a repeat recommendation of HIV testing in 6 weeks. Because the highest risk for transmission to the fetus is during delivery and postpartum breast-feeding, prenatal diagnosis is still of benefit to the patient and her fetus.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. Offer prophylactic zidovudine (AZT) therapy (Choice B) is inappropriate for this patient. AZT is most commonly used to treat active HIV infection in combination with other agents or intravenously during labor and delivery to prevent fetal transmission of the disease. However, it is unclear if this patient has HIV and thus treatment is not warranted. as adverse effects of AZT are sianificant and include cvtopenias. liver iniurv. mvopathv. and lactic acidosis. a this patient has HIV without an appropriate test, for which she has not given her consent. While she is at high risk for HIV infection, treatment with appropriate antiretroviral therapy could prevent fetal transmission. Perform HIV testing without her consent because she is at high risk for HIV (Choice D) is unethical. While this patient is at high risk for HIV infection, performing testing without her permission would be inappropriate and would damage her relationship with the clinician. Instead, the healthcare provider should evaluate the patient's reasons for refusing the HIV test. Refuse to treat the patient if she does not follow the recommended tests (Choice E) is inappropriate. Ending the patient-physician relationship would likely prevent the patient from receiving appropriate prenatal care, further increasing the risk of both maternal and fetal morbidity and mortality.</p>", "19": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Chlamydia trachomatis may cause urethritis that presents with urinary frequency, urgency, and dysuria in the setting of a normal urinalysis. Because it is an obligate intracellular pathogen, it will not appear on urinalysis or Gram stain, but is rather diagnosed with urethral culture or nucleic acid amplification tests. Treatment consists of either azithromycin or doxycycline in combination with ceftriaxone, with the latter covering a potential Neisseria gonorrhoeae coinfection.</p><p>A patient with symptoms of urethritis (eg, urinary frequency, urgency, and dysuria) and a urinalysis with no evidence of urinary tract infection raises concern for other causes of urethritis such as sexually transmitted infections secondary to Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae. Both organisms can cause symptoms of urethritis, and if left untreated, can progress to cervicitis, pelvic inflammatory disease, and tubo-ovarian abscess. In urinary tract infections, urinalysis typically shows the presence of white blood cells, bacteria, and positive leukocyte esterase and/or nitrites, all of which are absent in this patient. Chlamydia is an obligate intracellular pathogen and does not appear on urinalysis or Gram stain, resulting in absence of visualized bacteria on urinalysis. Diagnosing chlamydial urethritis involves urethral culture and/or nucleic acid amplification tests to evaluate the presence of Chlamydia. Treatment consists of either azithromycin or doxycycline in combination with ceftriaxone, with the latter covering a potential NV. gonorrhoeae coinfection.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E. Aewelovir (Choice A) is ised in the treatment of hernes nanitalic carised hv hernes cimnley viriie Genital hernes nresente with muiltinia small nainfill vesicles that arnde ta ~ cause shallow, round ulcers. It may be associated with fever and inguinal lymphadenopathy, as well as dysuria. However, it does not cause mucopurulent discharge. Fluconazole (Choice C) treats vulvovaginal candidiasis, most caused by Candida albicans. Candidiasis presents with vulvar erythema, burning, vaginal pruritus, and a thick, white vaginal discharge. It does not cause urethritis. Metronidazole (Choice D) is used to treat trichomoniasis and bacterial vaginosis. Trichomoniasis presents with pruritus, dysuria, dyspareunia, cervical erythema, and abnormal discharge that is described as green, frothy, and malodorous. In contrast, bacterial vaginosis causes gray, thin, malodorous discharge. Neither presents with urinary frequency, urgency, or dysuria. Zidovudine (AZT) (Choice E) is used in combination with other antiretroviral medications in the treatment of active HIV. Acute HIV infection commonly presents with a flu- like illness, consisting of fever, malaise, fatigue, lymphadenopathy, and a sore throat. While patients with HIV are at increased risk for infection, such as urinary tract infections, this patient's urinalysis shows no signs of infection. She also has no other symptoms of HIV, making zidovudine (AZT) treatment inappropriate.</p>", "20": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Ovarian cancer classically presents indolently with abdominal distension, early satiety, and weight loss in a postmenopausal woman. Signs of advanced disease include bowel obstruction, ascites, pleural effusions, and venous thromboembolism. The most common histology of ovarian cancer is an ovarian tumor of epithelial origin.</p><p>Ovarian cancer commonly presents indolently with abdominal distension, early satiety, and weight loss in postmenopausal women. Signs and symptoms of advanced disease may also include bowel obstruction with consequent nausea and vomiting, ascites, pleural effusions, and venous thromboembolism. In addition to these, physical examination may show a palpable adnexal mass on bimanual examination or lymphadenopathy. Ultrasonography is recommended to identify or characterize the responsible lesion, with concerning features including septations, cystic components, increased blood flow, and a heterogeneous structure. Further evaluation is then completed with tumor markers, commonly serum cancer antigen 125 (CA 125), and additional imaging to determine the extent of disease. Biopsy may be taken of omental or lymph node metastases prior to definitive surgical management, with most ovarian cancers being of epithelial origin. Risk factors for the development of ovarian cancer include a family history, genetic mutation (eg, BRCA), increased age, smoking, nulliparity, endometriosis, and pelvic radiation. Treatment includes surgical removal and chemotherapy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. Follicular cyst of the ovary (Choice A) is a simple cystic structure of the ovary that occurs during the process of follicle development when an egg is not appropriately released from a follicle during ovulation. Patients mav be asvmptomatic but can present with abdominal or pelvic pain if the cvst is larae. The mass seen in this patientis ~ by ectopic endometrial gland implantation. Dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and dyschezia are classic symptoms of endometriosis. This patient has no symptoms of endometriosis, and this subtype of ovarian cancer is much less common than epithelial carcinoma. Ovarian tumor of germ cell origin (Choice D) includes teratomas, dysgerminomas, yolk sac tumors, and mixed germ cell tumors. Broadly, they present with abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding, and bloating, as well as increased concentrations of hormones, such as β-hCG, alpha-fetoprotein, and lactate dehydrogenase, and a unilateral pelvic mass on physical examination. They are not typically associated with large, cystic masses of the ovary. Ovarian tumor of stromal origin (Choice E) includes sex cord-stromal tumors, such as granulosa cell tumors, theca cell tumors, and Sertoli-Leydig cell tumors. These tumors are hormonally active and commonly present with a palpable adnexal mass, abnormal uterine bleeding (especially in the case of the estrogen-producing granulosa cell tumor), or evidence of hyperandrogenism. They would be unlikely to cause symptoms solely from mass effect.</p>", "21": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Bilateral oophorectomy results in premature menopause due to the loss of ovarian estrogen production. It presents with typical manifestations of menopause such as vaginal atrophy or dryness, hot flashes, night sweats, loss of libido, and disturbances in mood or sleeping patterns. Patients undergoing elective oophorectomy should be counseled about an increased risk for osteoporosis due to estrogen’s role in bone mineralization and maintenance.</p><p>Menopause is caused by the lack of ovarian estrogen production. On average, it begins around 50 years of age due to a diminution of ovarian reserve but can occur earlier in premature ovarian failure caused by chemotherapy or radiation therapy toxicity, or by surgical removal of the ovaries. Manifestations include vaginal atrophy or dryness, hot flashes, night sweats, loss of libido, and disturbances in mood or sleeping patterns. Because sex hormones such as estrogen stimulate osteoblasts and increase or maintain bone mineral density, the loss of ovarian estrogen leads to an accelerated loss of bone mass after menopause, increasing the risk for developing osteopenia or osteoporosis. The condition is often asymptomatic until a traumatic injury results in a fracture, most commonly involving the hip, distal radius, or vertebrae. The diagnosis of osteoporosis is made using a DEXA scan once the bone mineral density falls below 2.5 standard deviations of the mean for a young adult. Symptoms of menopause can be treated with selective serotonin/norepinephrine inhibitors or with hormone replacement therapy (HRT) with supplemental estrogen since manifestations and sequelae are secondary to estrogen deficiency. The duration of estrogen supplementation should generally be limited to 3 to 5 years, as prolonged exposure to unopposed estrogen can increase the risk for endometrial cancer, cardiovascular disease and mortality, thromboembolism, and breast cancer.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D. Breast cancer (Choice A) risk is primarily related to the degree of lifetime estrogen exposure; factors such as early menarche, late menopause, nulliparity, obesity, and HRT significantly increase the risk for developing breast cancer. Surgical removal of the ovaries and premature menopause would decrease this patient's lifetime estrogen exposure and decrease her risk for breast cancer. Colon cancer (Choice B) risk factors include a low-fiber diet, excessive consumption of red or processed meats, obesity, tobacco use, and hereditary cancer syndromes such as familial adenomatous polyposis or hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer. Estrogen and progesterone deficiency after menopause has been linked to an increased risk for colon cancer, but data is mixed and this correlation is not as robust as the patient's known increased risk for osteoporosis with premature acceleration of postmenopausal bone loss. Dementia, Alzheimer type (Choice C) has a wide variety of risk factors including early menopause, as well as metabolic risk factors such as hypertension, diabetes mellitus, dyslipidemia, cerebrovascular disease, sedentary lifestyle, and brain trauma. However, this patient is at higher risk for developing osteoporosis given estrogen’s role in bone maintenance. Heart disease (Choice D) risk is not increased by menopause. Were this patient to request HRT to decrease her risk for developing osteoporosis, she must be counseled on its sequelae including increased risk for heart disease and thromboembolic events.</p>", "22": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Rh(D)-negative mothers are at a high risk for Rh(D) alloimmunization when exposed to Rh(D) antigens from the fetal circulation. Alloimmunization can be prevented by the administration of Rhg(D) immune globulin at 28 weeks’ gestation, following the delivery of an Rh(D)-positive newborn, with any invasive procedure (eg, amniocentesis), and with any episodes of fetomaternal bleeding. Rh0(D) immune globulin’s effect lasts approximately 12 weeks, so more than one dose may be required during pregnancy.</p><p>Rh(D)-negative mothers are at a high risk for developing anti-D antibodies if exposed to blood from an Rh(D)-positive fetus, which most commonly occurs during delivery. If Rh(D) antigens from the fetal circulation enter the maternal circulation, it is possible that the mother’s immune system will form antibodies against the Rh(D) antigen. While this carries no significant complication with the initial exposure, a subsequent pregnancy with an Rh(D)-positive fetus can result in severe hemolytic anemia of the newborn (erythroblastosis fetalis) as maternal antibodies cause hemolysis of fetal erythrocytes when they cross the placenta. Rh0(D) immune globulin acts by binding to Rh(D)- positive cells that enter the maternal circulation, preventing the immune system from developing antibodies to the Rh(D) antigen. Administration of Rhg(D) immune globulin is typically performed for Rh(D)-negative mothers at 28 weeks’ gestation, following the delivery of an Rh(D)-positive newborn, with any invasive procedure (eg, amniocentesis), and with any episodes of fetomaternal bleeding. Rh0(D) immune globulin is effective for approximately 12 weeks, so even though this patient has received Rh0(D) immune globulin earlier in pregnancy, enough time has passed that she should receive an additional dose now.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. 5 Measurement of cerebral artery flow by Doppler velocimetry (Choice A) is used in the evaluation of fetal anemia, often in conjunction with Doppler assessment of the umbilical artery. This patient is not currently at increased risk for fetal anemia as it is her first pregnancy and she received appropriate prophylaxis with Rh,(D) immune globulin during her amniocentesis. Additionally, measuring the cerebral artery flow will not prevent future Rh sensitization in this patient. Serum screening for atypical antibodies (Choice B) is not necessary at this time. Most patients will be evaluated with a type and screen when they present in labor, in preparation for the blood loss associated with birth. This test will screen for atypical antibodies, although this patient is not at increased risk for their presence as this is her first pregnancy and she has no history of blood transfusion. However, screening for them now is not necessary as it will not alter the management of her pregnancy or the outcome of her newborn. Amniocentesis for anti-D antibody (Choice D) is not recommended. Amniocentesis is an invasive sampling of amniotic fluid through the abdominal wall, usually performed at 15 to 20 weeks’ gestation, to evaluate for chromosomal or genetic abnormalities. The presence of anti-D antibodies in the amniotic fluid would indicate an increased risk for erythroblastosis fetalis if the baby was Rh-positive. However, performing amniocentesis on this patient at this time is dangerous to the fetus given their gestational age and would increase the risk for alloimmunization, rather than decrease it. No further treatment is indicated (Choice E) is inappropriate. Administration of Rhg(D) immune globulin is recommended after any invasive procedure, at 28 weeks’ gestation, and after delivery of an Rh(D)-positive newborn. While this patient received Rh0(D) immune globulin earlier in pregnancy, she should receive it again at 28 weeks’ gestation in preparation for delivery. If she does not, she is at risk for developing Rh(D) alloimmunization.</p>", "23": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: A ruptured ovarian cyst typically presents with severe, acute onset of unilateral pelvic pain, most commonly after sexual intercourse or physical activity, in an otherwise hemodynamically stable patient. Ultrasonography will show a simple or complex cystic ovarian mass or free fluid in the pelvis. Treatment consists of observation and supportive therapy.</p><p>There are multiple subtypes of ovarian cysts, ranging from benign simple cysts to complex, malignant cystic masses. The most common cysts are follicular and corpus luteal/nemorrhagic cysts. Follicular cysts are simple cystic structures of the ovary that occur during the process of follicle development when an egg is not appropriately released from a follicle during ovulation. Contrarily, a corpus luteum cyst, which can be hemorrhagic, may form following the formation of a mature follicle and subsequent ovulation during the menstrual cycle. Either of these ovarian cysts can potentially rupture, which classically presents with pelvic or lower abdominal pain that is sudden in onset and severe, most commonly after sexual intercourse or other physical activity. Physical examination may show a unilateral adnexal mass, which may be tender. Laboratory studies are typically within the reference ranges. Pelvic ultrasonography will show either a simple or complex cystic ovarian mass or free fluid in the pelvis if ruptured, which may include hemorrhagic components (eg, hemoperitoneum), particularly in the setting of a corpus luteal or hemorrhagic cyst. These cysts are typically managed with observation and supportive therapy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and F. and tenderness to palpation. It typically has a gradually worsening course over hours to days, whereas a ruptured ovarian cyst presents more acutely. Additionally, this patient has no fever or leukocytosis to suggest an infection. Endometrioma (Choice B) is a cyst that is formed from ectopic endometrial tissue within the adnexa in a patient with endometriosis (Choice C). If palpable on physical examination, it is often adherent to nearby structures. Endometriosis refers to the presence of ectopic endometrial tissue outside the uterus and commonly presents with dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, dyschezia, and infertility. This patient has no previous symptoms of endometriosis, and neither of these conditions present with the acute onset of severe pain. Intestinal colic (Choice D) refers to intermittent abdominal pain with associated nausea, emesis, diarrhea, anorexia, and bloating. It is more common in irritable bowel disease and inflammatory bowel diseases. This patient has no associated symptoms with her pain, and it has been constant rather than intermittent. Spontaneous abortion (Choice F) of a pregnancy presents with acute vaginal bleeding and cramping prior to 20 weeks’ gestation. There are various stages of spontaneous abortion depending on the presence or absence of a fetal heartbeat, whether the cervical os is open or closed, and the extent of fetal passage through the cervix. This patient has a negative serum β-hCG concentration, making spontaneous abortion incorrect.</p>", "24": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Antepartum intrauterine fetal demise is most commonly a result of chromosomal abnormalities, uteroplacental insufficiency, and maternal medical disease in high-income countries. Risk factors for uteroplacental insufficiency include pregestational or gestational diabetes, pregestational or gestational hypertension, preeclampsia, cocaine use, smoking, coagulopathies, severe anemia, placental abruption, and intrauterine infection. Patients with pregestational diabetes require tight glucose control during conception and pregnancy to minimize the effects of hyperglycemia on the fetus.</p><p>In high-income countries, stillbirth is most commonly a result of chromosomal abnormalities, uteroplacental insufficiency, and maternal medical disease. In low-income countries, it is more commonly a result of maternal infection, preeclampsia, or obstructed labor. Risk factors for uteroplacental insufficiency include pregestational or gestational diabetes, pregestational or gestational hypertension, preeclampsia, cocaine use, smoking, coagulopathies, severe anemia, placental abruption, and intrauterine infection. In this patient with a significant family history of diabetes mellitus, normotension, no history of drug use, no history of fetal insults, and no history of prior fetal abnormalities, occult pregestational diabetes should be suspected; screening can be performed with measurement of the patient's fasting serum glucose concentration. Patients with pregestational diabetes require tight glucose control during conception and pregnancy to minimize the effects of hyperglycemia on the fetus, including macrosomia and neonatal respiratory distress syndrome. After birth, the fetus still synthesizes excess insulin but is no longer exposed to the maternal blood glucose concentrations, which leads to hypoglycemia. Other complications associated with diabetes during pregnancy include congenital malformations (eg, cardiac, caudal - HARCRAMIRGN RERAAINGRAATN ientAeERlI Lie TAlbni AST AIRE ANEN EAR AH ARIA TEAC a ACRALARARIALR ARIE AIAG! INA CAAPACH ALITA REATH regression), preeclampsia, preterm labor, intrauterine growth restriction due to uteroplacental insufficiency, and maternal mortality.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. HLA typing of the patient and her husband (Choice A) is less likely to reveal the cause of her prior third trimester loss, as the degree of HLA mismatch between parents is primarily a risk factor for miscarriage earlier in pregnancy. Uteroplacental insufficiency is a far more likely cause of late-pregnancy loss, and occult diabetes should be suspected based on this patient's family history. Karyotype analysis of the patient and her husband (Choice B) could determine the presence of gonadal mosaicism or Robertsonian translocations that could predispose the fetus to chromosomal abnormalities. However, it is typically used when other evaluations are normal given the relatively low prevalence of abnormal parental karyotypes. In this patient, karyotype abnormality is less likely than pregestational diabetes. Semen analysis (Choice D) is useful in the evaluation of male infertility. Metrics include sperm count, motility, and morphology as well as volume and pH. If workup for maternal factors contributing to placental insufficiency is unrevealing and genetic testing of the parents is desired, paternal genetic testing should first be performed with somatic cells, not haploid germ cells. Hysterosalpingography (Choice E) is a radiographic technique used to determine the structure of the uterine cavity and fallopian tubes, including tube patency. It is useful in the evaluation of recurrent pregnancy loss, but an anatomic issue with the uterus or fallopian tubes is less likely than pregestational diabetes in this patient with a history of late third trimester pregnancy loss and strong family history of diabetes mellitus.</p>", "25": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Fibrocystic change of the breast is a benign condition occurring in menstruating women that presents with multiple, bilateral, painful breast cysts. Reexamination after menses should show a decrease in size of the fibrocystic changes, which distinguishes the diagnosis from other causes.</p><p>Fibrocystic changes of the breast occur most commonly in premenopausal women. Patients may present with various sites of breast pain and lumps, which may be bilateral. The condition is characterized by the presence of cysts, stromal fibrosis, and apocrine metaplasia on histology, and is believed to occur in response to hormonal fluctuations during the menstrual cycle. Reexamination after menses (eg, day 9 of the next menstrual cycle) may be helpful for diagnosis because the size of fibrocystic changes should subside after menses, distinguishing it from other causes. On examination, the cysts may appear blue. Fibrocystic change is a benign disease and does not generally convey an increased risk for cancer except for certain subtypes; however, it can result in recurrent mastalgia. If there is associated discomfort, aspiration can be performed, which may relieve symptoms. Cyst aspiration can yield fluid that is clear, milky, yellow, green, or brown, which are all benign. Supportive care is also appropriate, using nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and a well-fitting bra.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. Meaciirament of seriim nralactin cancentratian (Choice R) is iicefill in the avalitatinn af aalactarrhea and menetriial evele irrenularitiae (an aliaamenarrhea amenorrhea) — ~ because of its inhibition of gonadotropin-releasing hormone. Galactorrhea refers to milky discharge from one or both nipples, which this patient is not experiencing. Mammography (Choice C) is most used as a screening modality to detect early stages of breast cancer, although it may also be used to aid in the diagnosis of a palpable breast mass. However, ultrasonography is more commonly used to aid in diagnosis for patients under age 35 years, as their breast tissue is often too dense to be adequately evaluated with mammography. Ultrasonography of the breasts (Choice D) is frequently used in the evaluation of breast lesions for patients under age 35 years. It can determine the need for fine-needle aspiration (eg, breast cyst) or further imaging. This patient has no palpable breast lump, which would make ultrasonography ineffective in evaluating her symptoms. If performed, it would likely show multiple small cystic lesions. MRI of the breasts (Choice E) may be used for the evaluation of a breast lump in patients under age 35 years, but it is more commonly reserved for evaluation of patients with breast implants or those with a high risk for developing breast cancer (eg, BRCA mutation carriers). This patient may require additional imaging in the future, but she should first have a repeat examination on day 9 of her next menstrual cycle.</p>", "26": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Vaginal foreign bodies can present in prepubertal children with genital discomfort, vulvar erythema or pruritus, vaginal discharge, or vaginal bleeding. While sexual abuse should be considered, vaginal foreign bodies are more commonly due to a child’s curiosity and exploratory genital play.</p><p>Vaginal foreign bodies can present in prepubertal children with genital discomfort, vulvar erythema or pruritus, vaginal discharge, or vaginal bleeding. The most common foreign body is toilet paper, but other objects such as small toy parts and coins may be inserted due to the child’s curiosity and exploratory genital play. Diagnosis with visualization of the vaginal vault, saline irrigation, or examination under anesthesia may be required. Treatment requires removal of the foreign body.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. Adenocarcinoma of the vagina (Choice A) may be asymptomatic or present with vaginal bleeding or a vaginal mass. The primary risk factor for its development includes exposure to diethylstilbestrol in utero. However, this most commonly presents after the onset of menarche, making this patient likely too young for its development. Candidiasis (Choice B) classically presents with vaginal pruritus, vulvar erythema, and a thick, white vaginal discharge. It is caused by the overgrowth of Candida species, most commonly Candida albicans. |t would be unlikely to present in a patient this young and would not commonly cause bloody vaginal discharge. Sarcoma botryoides (Choice D) refers to rhabdomyosarcoma of the vagina. It often presents during early childhood with soft nodules at the vaginal entrance that have the appearance of a cluster of grapes. It does not cause vaginal discomfort, bleeding, or foul-smelling discharge unless advanced or complicated by infection or invasion of Y Trauma (Choice E) can cause vaginal bleeding in a child. Most commonly this occurs due to blunt trauma from a fall, and, less frequently, from penetrating trauma. Sexual trauma can also cause vaginal bleeding or foul-smelling discharge if a concomitant sexually transmitted infection is present. This patient has no evidence or history of obvious trauma.</p>", "27": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Hydatidiform moles commonly present with first trimester vaginal bleeding, abdominal pain, uterine size greater than expected for dates, and persistent nausea and vomiting. Symptoms of hyperthyroidism may also be seen, as increased β-hCG concentrations can stimulate the thyroid to secrete thyroid hormone. Amolar pregnancy can progress to malignant trophoblastic disease in the form of invasive moles or choriocarcinoma.</p><p>Hydatidiform moles are caused by either the fertilization of an empty egg by a single sperm followed by duplication of the paternal DNA resulting in 46 chromosomes (complete) or the dual fertilization of a normal ovum resulting in 69 chromosomes (incomplete). Maternal symptoms include first trimester vaginal bleeding, abdominal pain, uterine size greater than expected for dates, and hyperemesis gravidarum or persistent nausea/vomiting, the latter related to abnormally increased human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG) concentrations. The vaginal bleeding may be significant and cause anemia with associated hypotension and tachycardia (eg, hemorrhagic shock, as seen in this case). Additionally, increased concentrations of β-hCG can stimulate the thyroid to secrete thyroid hormone, leading to symptoms of hyperthyroidism, such as ~</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, E, F, and G. Abruptio placentae (Choice A) is a premature separation of the placenta from the uterine endometrium that occurs after 20 weeks’ gestation. Presenting findings include abdominal or pelvic pain, abnormal vaginal bleeding, and tetanic contractions. This patient is only at 12 weeks’ gestation, making abruptio placentae unlikely. Ectopic pregnancy (Choice B) presents with vaginal bleeding and abdominal pain as a result of abnormal implantation of an embryo outside of the uterine cavity, commonly in the fallopian tube. It can also present with hemodynamic instability and hemoperitoneum in the setting of rupture. However, it would not likely be asymptomatic for 12 weeks prior to causing symptoms. Endometritis (Choice C) is an acute, typically polymicrobial, infection of the uterine endometrium. Patients typically present with fever, tenderness of the uterine fundus, and mucopurulent vaginal discharge. This patient has no uterine tenderness, fever, or mucopurulent vaginal discharge, which decreases the likelihood of endometritis as the cause of her symptoms. Placenta accreta (Choice E) refers to the abnormal attachment of the placenta to the myometrium, rather than just the endometrium. Risk factors include prior cesarean delivery or uterine surgery, increased maternal age, and multiparity. It is typically asymptomatic until delivery, when it causes postpartum hemorrhage as a result of retained products of conception. Uterine rupture (Choice F) occurs when the muscular wall of the uterus tears and presents with severe abdominal pain, hemodynamic instability, and loss of fetal station. Risk factors include previous cesarean delivery and induction of labor, and treatment consists of emergent laparotomy. Uterine rupture does not present spontaneously in early pregnancy with vaginal bleeding. Vasa previa (Choice G) is associated with placenta previa but refers specifically to the condition in which blood vessels overlie the cervical os. While commonly diagnosed on routine screening ultrasonography, symptoms of an unrecognized vasa previa include vaginal bleeding with rupture of membranes or sexual intercourse that can lead to fetal exsanguination. This patient is still early in pregnancy and her membranes remain intact, making vasa previa unlikely.</p>", "28": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Assisted reproduction, such as ovulation-induction, intrauterine insemination, and in vitro fertilization, can lead to multiple gestation pregnancy. These pregnancies are most commonly diagnosed on prenatal ultrasonography but may be suspected earlier because of persistent nausea and vomiting or uterine size larger than expected dates. Complications include preterm labor, intrauterine fetal growth restriction, twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome, and fetal malpresentation.</p><p>Multiple gestation pregnancy occurs when more than one ovum is fertilized and implants in the endometrial lining of the uterine cavity. While this can occur spontaneously, it is more common with reproductive technologies, such as ovulation-induction, intrauterine insemination, and in vitro fertilization. Clomiphene use, as in this patient, stimulates ovulation by blocking the negative feedback on gonadotropin-releasing hormone in the hypothalamus via acting as an estrogen receptor antagonist. Because it nonspecifically stimulates ovulation, more than one egg can be produced and released during an ovulatory cycle, thus possibly leading to a multiple gestation pregnancy. Multiple gestation pregnancy is most commonly diagnosed on routine prenatal ultrasonography but may be suspected earlier because of hyperemesis gravidarum secondary to increased β-hCG concentration or a uterine size larger than expected for the current gestational age. Complications of multiple gestation pregnancy include preterm labor, intrauterine fetal growth restriction, twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome, and fetal malpresentation.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. Hydatidiform mole (Choice A) commonly presents with persistent nausea and vomiting, first trimester vaginal bleeding, early preeclampsia, and uterine size out of proportion to expected gestational age because of an abnormal fertilization of an ovum. Pelvic ultrasonography will show diffuse echogenic structures in the uterus and bilateral ovarian theca-lutein cysts. They are precursors to malignant trophoblastic disease, such as choriocarcinoma. Assistive reproductive technologies, such as clomiphene, do not increase the risk for hydatidiform mole. Y Polyhydramnios (Choice D) refers to an excess of amniotic fluid that is associated with fetal developmental defects that result in impaired swallowing, such as anencephaly, esophageal atresia, or duodenal atresia. Additional risk factors include maternal diabetes mellitus, maternofetal Rh incompatibility, and congenital infections. While it may cause enlargement of the uterus on physical examination, it would be unlikely to cause profound nausea and vomiting this early in pregnancy. Incorrect dates (Choice E) may cause inaccuracies in expected uterine size and β-hCG concentrations. Gestational age is typically determined by the patient’s last menstrual period or by ultrasonography early in the pregnancy. However, incorrect dates would not explain this patient's prolonged nausea and vomiting.</p>", "29": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Uterine inversion is a rare, life-threatening complication of childbirth that occurs when the uterine fundus inverts into the endometrial cavity or vagina. It presents with abdominal pain, severe vaginal bleeding, absence of the fundus in the abdomen, and the presence of a fleshy mass at the cervix or in the vagina. As inversion prevents appropriate uterine contraction, it can cause massive postpartum hemorrhage and hypovolemic shock. Treatment includes fluid and blood resuscitation, the administration of uterine relaxants, and the immediate replacement of the uterus into its appropriate position.</p><p>Uterine inversion is a rare, life-threatening complication of childbirth. It most commonly occurs during vaginal delivery when excessive traction is applied to the umbilical cord as the placenta is being delivered. This results in inversion of the uterine fundus into the endometrial cavity, with severe cases presenting with the uterine fundus at or beyond the introitus. Risk factors include macrosomia, preeclampsia, uterine structural abnormalities, and placenta accreta, the latter of which is likely present in this patient given her history of cesarean deliveries and adherent placenta. Classically, uterine inversion presents with abdominal pain and severe vaginal bleeding. Physical examination will show absence of the fundus at the umbilicus, but rather it will be present on visual examination of the cervix and vagina. As the inverted uterus is unable to appropriately contract, uterine atony persists, and the patient is at risk for massive postpartum hemorrhage, leading to hemodynamic instability and hypovolemic shock. Treatment includes hemodynamic support and resuscitation, the administration of uterine relaxants, and immediate replacement of the uterus into its appropriate position. Delaying replacement can lead to edema and vascular congestion of the uterus, preventing further attempts at replacement. For this reason, if initial attempts are unsuccessful, emergent laparotomy should be performed.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E. hemorrhage secondary to uterine atony. It increases smooth muscle contraction and muscular tone, which is beneficial in uterine atony but would be detrimental in the management of uterine inversion. Because the uterus needs to remain relaxed in order to be replaced and prevent severe bleeding, uterotonics should not be administered. Curettage of placental remnants from the visible inverted uterus (Choice C) or careful dissection of the placenta from the visible inverted uterus (Choice D) are inappropriate. This patient likely has some degree of placenta accreta, or invasion of the placenta into the myometrium, which prevents it from appropriately separating from the uterus. Placenta accreta is most commonly treated with hysterectomy to prevent further bleeding. However, leaving the uterus inverted to try to remove the placenta causes persistent uterine atony, ongoing severe postpartum hemorrhage, hypovolemic shock, and uterine edema that will prevent replacement. The uterus should be replaced as quickly as possible. Transvaginal hysterectomy (Choice E) may be required for this patient in order to adequately remove the placenta. However, the more appropriate immediate step in the treatment of this patient is to replace the uterus into its appropriate position to minimize the risk for postpartum hemorrhage.</p>", "30": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Excessive vitamin A administration during pregnancy is teratogenic, leading to microcephaly, cardiac defects, and spontaneous abortion.</p><p>Vitamin A is a fat-soluble vitamin found in a variety of foods, such as leafy green vegetables, carrots, and egg yolks. It plays an important role in the maintenance of the cornea and conjunctiva, as well as phototransduction. As such, vitamin A deficiency can lead to night blindness and xerophthalmia. Alternatively, vitamin A toxicity can cause nausea, vomiting, and changes in vision when the toxicity is acute, or ataxia, hepatotoxicity, and alopecia when the toxicity is chronic. However, in pregnant patients, excessive ingestion of vitamin A can lead to teratogenic effects in the fetus. These include microcephaly and cardiac defects, as well as spontaneous abortion. This patient is likely already receiving a sufficient daily dose of vitamin A with her prenatal vitamin and vegan diet and should thus decrease the other forms of her ingested vitamin A, such as the daily supplement, to avoid fetal toxicity.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. Moderation of her exercise routine (Choice A) is not necessary. Overall, exercise in pregnancy is considered safe and healthy. Beginning or continuing a program of moderate intensity aerobic and strength exercises, such as this patient's yoga routine, is an appropriate recommendation. However, the patient should avoid high intensity exercises or exercises with a risk for falls (eg, gymnastics) to avoid trauma to the abdomen and fetus. Targeted ultrasonography because of exposure to tetanus toxoid (Choice B) is not recommended. The tetanus toxoid vaccine is an inactivated vaccine that is safe to give during pregnancy and should be given without concern for risk to the pregnant patient or the fetus. Decreasing her vitamin B, intake (Choice D) is not necessary in this patient. Vitamin B, (thiamine) is a water-soluble vitamin that does not cause toxicity, even in excess. Deficiency can lead to beriberi or Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, and it is thus appropriate for this patient to continue her supplementation. MMR vaccine (Choice E) is inappropriate in a pregnant patient, as it is a live attenuated virus. Live vaccines are contraindicated in pregnant patients as they can lead to infection of the fetus. Rubella infection of an unvaccinated pregnant patient can lead to fetal defects such as sensorineural deafness, neurodevelopmental delay, congenital cardiac defects, microcephaly, and extramedullary hematopoiesis. Therefore, this patient should be vaccinated after delivery.</p>", "31": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Acute hyponatremia can present with seizures and altered mental status due to cerebral edema. In pregnant patients, the administration of hypotonic fluids and oxytocin, the latter of which induces antidiuretic hormone activity, can lead to significant hyponatremia. While this may be confused with eclampsia, concurrent signs of preeclampsia will not be present. Treatment consists of hypertonic saline.</p><p>Hyponatremia, or a serum sodium concentration less than 135 mEq/L, may be acute or chronic, with symptoms related to the severity of depletion and the duration of onset. Acute decreases in sodium concentration may lead to neurologic symptoms, such as seizure and altered mental status due to cerebral edema. Chronic hyponatremia, in contrast, leads to less severe symptoms, such as nausea, vomiting, light-headedness, fatigue, and confusion. Causes of acute hyponatremia include the administration of hypotonic fluids, syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH), kidney dysfunction, adrenal insufficiency, beer potomania, psychogenic polydipsia, and hydrochlorothiazide diuretics, among others. This patient received a significant volume of hypotonic solution, 5% dextrose in water, which likely decreased her sodium concentration. Additionally, large amounts of oxytocin can induce SIADH, with consequent water reabsorption. Patients who present with acute neurologic signs due to hyponatremia should be treated with hypertonic saline.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D. Amniotic fluid embolism (Choice A) classically occurs during deliverv or immediatelv post partum as a result of amniotic fluid entering the maternal circulation. It tvpicallv a collapse, and maternal and/or fetal death. It would not be expected to present with an isolated seizure. Eclampsia (Choice B) refers to the development of generalized tonic-clonic seizures in patients with preeclampsia. Preeclampsia presents with hypertension, proteinuria, and end-organ dysfunction after 20 weeks of gestation due to abnormalities of the placental spiral arteries. This patient has no reported symptoms of preeclampsia, which makes hyponatremia the more likely cause of her seizure. Epilepsy (Choice C) refers to a condition in which a patient experiences repeat seizures. It may be idiopathic, genetic, structural, or caused by a head injury. This patient has no history of previous seizures, making epilepsy unlikely. Hypocalcemia (Choice D) commonly presents with circumoral numbness, paresthesias, and muscle cramps, although it may also be asymptomatic. In severe cases, seizures and prolongation of the QT interval can occur. However, hypocalcemia more commonly occurs because of hypoparathyroidism or kidney dysfunction, not the administration of hypotonic fluid.</p>", "32": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Lactational mastitis presents with fever and unilateral erythematous, indurated, edematous, warm, tender breast tissue. It classically occurs in breast-feeding patients as a result of nipple excoriation and subsequent inoculation with cutaneous or oral flora. Treatment includes oral antibiotics and continuation of breast-feeding. Sequelae of lactational mastitis include early cessation of breast-feeding (due to pain) and progression to a breast abscess.</p><p>Mastitis (cellulitis of the breast tissue) presents with unilateral erythematous, indurated, edematous, warm, tender breast tissue, and is frequently associated with fever. It classically occurs in breast-feeding patients (also termed lactational mastitis) as a result of nipple inoculation with cutaneous or oral flora. Nipple excoriation or fissures from breast-feeding are a strong predisposing factor for mastitis as compromise of the skin barrier facilitates passage of organisms into the tissue. Secondary risk factors include incomplete breast emptying (Choice B), relative immune compromise (Choice E) from maternal stress, malnutrition (Choice A), lack of sleep, clogged duct, prior episodes of mastitis, tight-fitting bras, and use of antifungal nipple creams. Staphylococcus aureus is a common causative pathogen; others include coagulase-negative staphylococci, Streptococcus pyogenes, and Escherichia coli. Lactational mastitis is treated with oral antibiotics and continuation of breast-feeding. Sequelae of lactational mastitis include early cessation of breast-feeding (due to pain) and progression to a breast abscess. A thorough history and physical examination are important to rule out inflammatory breast carcinoma, which can present similarly with unilateral breast edema, erythema, and pain, as well as a characteristic ‘peau d’orange’ (orange peel) appearance of the skin. = Incomplete breast emptying (Choice B) caused by infrequent feeding, milk oversupply, or a clogged duct is a risk factor for lactational mastitis, as stagnant milk can provide a substrate for pathogens to grow. However, nipple excoriation, which facilitates inoculation of pathogens into the breast, is a stronger risk factor. Newborn thrush (Choice C) has not been shown to increase the risk for lactational mastitis. However, maternal use of antifungal cream on the nipples does increase the tisk for developing mastitis. Relative immune compromise (Choice E) caused by maternal illness, malnutrition, stress, or fatigue can impair healing of nipple trauma and impair immune response to pathogens introduced into the nipple. However, compromise of the skin barrier via nipple excoriation facilitates pathogen entry and is a stronger risk factor.</p>", "33": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Uterine rupture is a dangerous obstetric complication where the uterine wall tears. Acute pain, loss of fetal station, and both maternal and fetal hemodynamic instability are suggestive findings. Treatment is with emergent laparotomy.</p><p>Uterine rupture is a dangerous complication of pregnancy wherein the muscular wall of the uterus tears. Severe pain is a common initial finding. Hemodynamic instability can manifest in the mother with tachycardia and hypotension, and in the fetus with late decelerations, tachycardia, bradycardia, or loss of beat-to-beat variability. If uterine rupture occurs during labor, sudden loss of fetal station is a highly suggestive finding. Fetal parts may be palpable above the fundus with Leopold maneuvers. Risk factors for uterine rupture include vaginal birth after caesarean section, multiparity, and induction of labor with prostaglandin analogues or oxytocin. Uterine rupture is treated with emergent laparotomy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D. = mother can be associated with bradycardia on fetal heart tracing. This patient's blood pressure is within normal limits. Additionally, ephedrine would further increase this patient’s heart rate. Magnesium sulfate tocolysis (Choice B) is used to delay preterm labor for approximately 48 hours. Magnesium sulfate therapy is also used in the treatment of preeclampsia. Tocolysis prevents the uterus from further contractions, which can inhibit cervical change in laboring patients. However, tocolysis would be ineffective in managing this patient's condition as her contractions are not the cause of the fetal bradycardia. Terbutaline therapy (Choice C) is used to delay preterm labor and to treat oxytocin-induced uterine tachysystole, the latter of which can inhibit blood flow to the placenta and consequently the fetus. This decrease in blood supply causes fetal bradycardia. This patient's fetal bradycardia is not secondary to tachysystole or uterine contractions, making terbutaline therapy an incorrect answer. Forceps delivery (Choice D) is often used for a prolonged second stage of labor or in the setting of fetal distress. It requires a completely dilated and effaced cervix, ruptured membranes, and an engaged fetal occiput. As this patient is not completely dilated and has had sudden loss of fetal station, laparotomy is the most appropriate next step in management.</p>", "34": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Abruptio placentae refers to the early separation of the placenta from the uterus and is an obstetric emergency because of compromised fetal blood supply. It most commonly presents in the third trimester with vaginal bleeding, severe uterine pain, and tetanic contractions. Risk factors include preeclampsia, lower abdominal trauma, smoking, and cocaine use. Treatment includes fluid resuscitation and emergency cesarean delivery.</p><p>Abruptio placentae, or placental abruption, refers to the premature separation of the placenta from the uterus. It most commonly presents with vaginal bleeding and severe uterine pain. This patient also has severe preeclampsia; laboratory abnormalities include proteinuria, thrombocytopenia, and increased liver enzymes. In addition to vaginal bleeding on physical examination, patients with placental abruption will show uterine tenderness to palpation and abdominal rigidity. Because of the compromised fetal blood supply, it is an obstetric emergency. Treatment includes supportive care, fluid resuscitation, emergency cesarean delivery, and Rh0(D) immune globulin if the maternal blood type is Rh(D)-negative.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. Bloody show (Choice B) is a scant amount of vaginal bleeding or blood-tinged discharge that occurs several hours to 1 day prior to the onset of labor as the cervix begins to dilate and efface. This patient is having brisk bleeding associated with severe uterine pain despite irregular uterine contractions, which makes placental abruption a more likely diagnosis than bloody show. Disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) (Choice C) may present with vaginal bleeding, in addition to diffuse bleeding from other sites (eg, mouth, gums, intravenous access sites). Laboratory studies would show thrombocytopenia, prolongation of partial thromboplastin and prothrombin times, increased D-dimer concentrations, and decreased fibrinogen. In pregnant people, it is most commonly related to blood loss associated with placental abruption or postpartum hemorrhage, amniotic fluid embolism, and sepsis. This patient has no other signs of bleeding and no coagulation studies to determine whether DIC is present, making placental abruption the most likely immediate cause of her vaginal bleeding. Placenta previa (Choice D) refers to the abnormal positioning of placental tissue such that the placenta partially or completely overlies the internal cervical os. It most commonly presents with painless third trimester vaginal bleeding. Risk factors include multiparity, previous cesarean delivery, and prior dilatation and curettage. This patient has vaginal bleeding associated with severe uterine pain, making placental abruption a more likely diagnosis. Vasa previa (Choice E) is associated with placenta previa but refers specifically to the condition in which blood vessels overlie the cervical os. Though commonly found on routine screening ultrasonography, symptoms of an unrecognized vasa previa include vaginal bleeding with rupture of membranes. Damage to the fetal blood vessels results in significant fetal hemorrhage and possible exsanguination and death. This patient has severe pain associated with her vaginal bleeding and has yet to rupture her membranes, making vasa previa a less likely diagnosis.</p>", "35": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Bartholin abscesses present with a large, tender, fluctuant area over the posterolateral vaginal entrance. These abscesses are commonly polymicrobial bacterial infections with organisms originating from both the skin and enteric flora. Treatment includes incision and drainage, as well as antibiotic therapy.</p><p>A Bartholin abscess can occur when a Bartholin gland becomes obstructed and infected. A Bartholin gland is located on each side of the introitus at the 5- and 7-o’clock positions relative to the opening of the vagina. These glands produce secretions that lubricate the vagina. Occasionally, the ducts of these glands become obstructed, leading to a Bartholin cyst. This presents as a small area of painful, fluctuant swelling over the posterior aspect of the labium majus. With ongoing obstruction and inflammation, infection can develop, leading to a Bartholin abscess, marked by erythema and tenderness to palpation of the cyst. Abscesses are commonly polymicrobial bacterial infections with organisms originating from both the skin and enteric flora. If a single organism is identified, it is usually Neisseria gonorrhoeae, but this is not as common as a polymicrobial source. Bartholin abscesses are treated with incision and drainage, often with placement of a catheter to promote complete evacuation, and generally also with antibiotic administration.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. Fungal infection (Choice B) would be unlikely to cause a Bartholin abscess. Fungal infection of the vulva is typically caused by Candida species and presents with vulvar erythema, vaginal pruritus, and a thick white vaginal discharge. It does not cause a fluctuant mass near the introitus. Neoplasia (Choice C) may occur on the vulva near the vaginal entrance in the setting of vulvar carcinoma. This cancer commonly presents with a pruritic patch on the vulva, rather than the large fluctuant mass found in this patient. Trauma (Choice D) could theoretically cause a hematoma of the vulva or vagina. Hematomas classically present with a tender, fluctuant, discolored mass. This patient has no history of trauma, making a hematoma in this area less likely than a Bartholin abscess. Viral infection (Choice E) can cause abnormalities of the vulva. For example, infection with herpes simplex virus can cause small, painful, vesicular lesions. Viral infections are not causative organisms in Bartholin abscesses.</p>", "36": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Corpus luteum cysts are common in pregnancy as the corpus luteum must enlarge to support a growing pregnancy. These cysts can potentially rupture, which presents with acute onset and severe unilateral pelvic or lower abdominal pain. Ultrasonography may show a complex cystic mass or free fluid in the pelvis. They are treated with observation and supportive therapy.</p><p>The corpus luteum develops from the mature follicle after the egg has ovulated during the menstrual cycle. During pregnancy, it enlarges and secretes progesterone to maintain an early pregnancy until the placenta begins to synthesize it. This enlarged corpus luteum can form a cyst, which can potentially rupture. A ruptured corpus luteum cyst classically presents with unilateral pelvic or lower abdominal pain that is sudden in onset and severe, most commonly after sexual intercourse or other physical activity. Physical examination may show a unilateral adnexal mass, which may be tender. However, if the cyst has completely collapsed, it will not be evident on physical examination. Pelvic ultrasonography will show a complex cystic mass or free fluid in the pelvis if ruptured that may include hemorrhagic components (eg, hemoperitoneum). Culdocentesis, though rarely performed, typically produces serosanguineous fluid as the corpus luteum is highly vascular. These cysts are typically managed with observation and supportive therapy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. Ectanie nreananey (Choice R) acciire aiiteide af the andametrial cavity aften within a fallonian tihe It ie aevmntamatic intil larae ar rintired which nrecente with acite o Ectopic pregnancy (Choice B) occurs outside of the endometrial cavity, often within a fallopian tube. It is asymptomatic until large or ruptured, which presents with acute, severe abdominopelvic pain, vaginal bleeding, and signs of peritoneal inflammation. It is diagnosed with β-hCG testing and ultrasonographic evidence of an abnormally located pregnancy. This patient has an intrauterine yolk sac, making a normal intrauterine pregnancy with an associated corpus luteum cyst most likely. Endometrioma (Choice C) is a cyst that is formed from endometrial tissue within the adnexa in a patient with endometriosis. If palpable on physical examination, it is often adherent to nearby structures. Endometriosis commonly presents with dysmenorrhea, dyspareunia, and dyschezia. This patient has no previous symptoms of endometriosis, and endometriomas do not present with acute pain, making it unlikely in this patient. Hydatidiform mole (Choice D) commonly presents with persistent nausea and vomiting, first trimester vaginal bleeding, early preeclampsia, and uterine size out of proportion to the expected gestational age. While β-hCG would be increased, as it is in this patient, ultrasonography would typically show diffuse echogenic structures in the uterus and bilateral ovarian theca-lutein cysts. Ovarian germ cell tumor (Choice E) includes teratomas, dysgerminomas, yolk sac tumors, and mixed germ cell tumors. Broadly, ovarian germ cell tumors present with abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding, and bloating; increased concentrations of hormones such as β-hCG, alpha-fetoprotein, and lactate dehydrogenase; and a unilateral pelvic mass on physical examination. While this patient does have increased β-hCG, this is secondary to her normal intrauterine pregnancy, rather than an ovarian germ cell tumor.</p>", "37": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Endometritis refers to inflammation of the lining of the uterus, which may be secondary to infection, pelvic radiation therapy, IUDs, fibroids, or polyps. Chronic endometritis is commonly idiopathic and presents with abdominopelvic pain and abnormal uterine bleeding in addition to possible uterine or cervical motion tenderness on examination. Plasma cells in the endometrium are consistent with this disease.</p><p>Endometritis refers to inflammation or infection of the uterine lining. It may be acute or chronic, with acute endometritis most commonly being caused by pelvic inflammatory disease (PID). PID is characterized by inflammation of the upper genital tract and pelvis and presents with fever, abdominopelvic pain, and mucopurulent vaginal discharge, as well as cervical motion and uterine tenderness on examination. Ascending Chlamydia trachomatis or Neisseria gonorrhoeae infections are common pathogens involved in the development of PID. Chronic endometritis, in contrast, is most commonly idiopathic or inflammatory. Less common causes include pelvic radiation therapy, infection, IUDs, fibroids, and polyps. Presenting symptoms include abnormal uterine bleeding and abdominal pain, with physical examination disclosing uterine or cervical motion tenderness, although physical examination may also be unremarkable. Abnormal uterine bleeding refers to bleeding that is irregular, unpredictable, or not associated with usual menstrual signs and symptoms. If an endometrial biopsy is done for evaluation of abnormal bleeding in chronic endometritis, it will show plasma cells in the endometrium, as in this patient. Treatment of chronic endometritis is dependent on the underlying cause but may include antibiotic therapy, a Both endometrial cancer (Choice B) and endometrial hyperplasia (Choice C) commonly present with postmenopausal vaginal bleeding, although they may present with abnormal uterine bleeding in premenopausal women. If the patient is over age 45 years or has a history of prolonged estrogen exposure, this bleeding should be evaluated with an endometrial biopsy to rule out endometrial cancer. In hyperplasia, the biopsy will show an increased density of glands, mitotic cells, and atypical nuclei. In contrast, endometrial biopsy in endometrial cancer will show malignant cells with stromal invasion. Neither would present with plasma cells on biopsy. Hypergonadotropic hypogonadism (Choice D), or primary hypogonadism, often refers to premature ovarian failure. Premature ovarian failure presents in reproductive-age women with anovulation, amenorrhea, and infertility. Laboratory studies show decreased estrogen with increased follicle-stimulating hormone and luteinizing hormone concentrations. It more commonly occurs in patients younger than age 40 years and would be unlikely to cause abdominal pain. Hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (Choice E) may be the cause of anovulation or amenorrhea. In these patients, measurements of follicle-stimulating hormone and luteinizing hormone concentrations will be decreased. Causes include but are not limited to systemic illness, functional hypothalamic amenorrhea, and hypothalamic tumors. Plasma cells in the endometrium are consistent with chronic endometritis, not hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. Pelvic inflammatory disease (Choice F) is a common cause of acute endometritis and presents with fever, abdominopelvic pain, and mucopurulent discharge. Physical examination commonly discloses uterine, adnexal, or cervical motion tenderness. Endometrial biopsy in the setting of PID would show neutrophils, not plasma cells, within the endometrium.</p>", "38": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Vulvar hematomas may develop after delivery, especially in nulliparous patients with an extended second stage of labor or operative vaginal delivery. They present with painful, fluctuant, discolored masses on the labia. They are most commonly caused by injury to the pudendal artery. Treatment includes ice packs and analgesia, as well as incision and drainage, if necessary.</p><p>Pregnant patients are at risk for the development of puerperal hematomas during delivery. These hematomas may occur in the vulva, vagina, or retroperitoneum. Risk factors include nulliparity, macrosomia, operative vaginal deliveries, an extended second stage of labor, bleeding diatheses, and preeclampsia. Vulvar hematomas are most commonly caused by injuries to the pudendal artery. In contrast, vaginal and retroperitoneal hematomas are caused by injuries to the uterine and hypogastric arteries, respectively. Presentations also vary, with vulvar hematomas presenting with fluctuant, painful, discolored masses, as in this patient, vaginal hematomas causing rectal pressure, and retroperitoneal hematomas exhibiting hemodynamic instability. Treatment of vulvar and vaginal hematomas is often supportive with ice packs and analgesia but may require incision and drainage. Retroperitoneal hematomas require surgical intervention.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E. . gluteal region depending on the location of injury. It does not cause vulvar hematomas. Superior vesical (Choice C) artery is also a branch of the internal iliac artery. It supplies blood to the bladder and ureters, as well as the ductus deferens and seminal gland in men. It is unlikely to be injured in normal vaginal delivery. Uterine (Choice D) artery is a branch of the internal iliac artery that supplies blood to the uterus. Uterine artery injuries may be caused by operative vaginal delivery. These injuries more commonly lead to vaginal, rather than vulvar, hematomas. Vaginal (Choice E) artery supplies blood flow to the vagina in women. It arises from the internal iliac artery and anastomoses with a branch of the uterine artery. Vaginal artery injuries may cause vaginal hematomas but would be unlikely to cause a vulvar hematoma.</p>", "39": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea occurs in patients with inadequate caloric intake, increased energy expenditure, or excessive levels of emotional stress. It is caused by an inhibition of gonadotropin-releasing hormone, which results in irregular secretion of gonadotropin and decreased concentrations of follicle-stimulating hormone, luteinizing hormone, and estrogen production. Other causes of amenorrhea, such as pregnancy, should be ruled out in these patients.</p><p>Secondary amenorrhea may be caused by pregnancy, menopause or premature ovarian failure, ovulatory dysfunction, abnormalities of the hypothalamic-pituitary-ovarian axis, inadequate nutrition, chromosomal abnormalities, and thyroid disorders. Functional hypothalamic amenorrhea refers to amenorrhea that occurs in the setting of increased stress or decreased energy availability, which may be secondary to inadequate nutrition or over-exercising, often in a patient with a low BMI. In order to diagnose functional hypothalamic amenorrhea, pregnancy and other pathologies should first be excluded. It is caused by a decrease in gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH), which consequently causes irregular secretion of gonadotropin and decreased follicle-stimulating hormone, luteinizing hormone, ovulation, and estrogen production. Hypercortisolemia, caused by stress, may also play a role in the inhibition of GnRH.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D. Abnormal peripheral estrogen conversion (Choice A) is incorrect. Estrogens are converted to androgens in peripheral adipose tissue, which can occur in excess in patients with polycystic ovary syndrome. However, this patient has a low BMI, indicating she has minimal peripheral adipose tissue for estrogen conversion. Decrease in serum cortisol concentration (Choice B) is incorrect. In contrast, patients with functional hypothalamic amenorrhea commonly have increased concentrations of cortisol, which leads to negative feedback on the release of GnRH. This then causes decreased follicle-stimulating hormone and luteinizing hormone concentrations, which consequently leads to anovulation and amenorrhea. Increase in serum prolactin concentration (Choice C) can occur in hyperprolactinemia, which can cause amenorrhea due to inhibition of GnRH, follicle-stimulating hormone, and luteinizing hormone. More commonly than amenorrhea, however, it presents with galactorrhea, which this patient is not experiencing. Increase in serum thyroid-stimulating hormone concentration (Choice D) is seen in primary hypothyroidism, which most frequently presents with fatigue, weight gain, constipation, cold intolerance, and oligomenorrhea. It may also cause infertility due to anovulation. This patient has no other signs of hypothyroidism, such as weight gain, making this an unlikely cause of her amenorrhea.</p>", "40": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Ovarian cancer classically presents indolently with abdominal distension, early satiety, and weight loss in a postmenopausal woman. Signs of advanced disease include bowel obstruction, ascites, pleural effusions, and venous thromboembolism. Ultrasonography is the diagnostic imaging modality of choice and will show an ovarian mass that is heterogeneous with septations, as well as both cystic and solid components. Treatment includes surgical excision and chemotherapy.</p><p>Ovarian cancer commonly presents indolently with abdominal distension, early satiety, and weight loss in postmenopausal women. Signs and symptoms of advanced disease may also include bowel obstruction with consequent nausea and vomiting, ascites, pleural effusions, and venous thromboembolism. In addition to these, physical examination may show a palpable adnexal mass on bimanual examination or lymphadenopathy. Ultrasonography is recommended to identify or characterize the responsible lesion, with concerning features including septations, cystic components, blood flow, and a heterogeneous structure. Further evaluation is completed with tumor markers, commonly serum cancer antigen 125 (CA 125), and additional imaging to determine the extent of disease. Risk factors for the development of ovarian cancer include a family history, genetic mutation (eg, BRCA), increased age, smoking, nulliparity, and pelvic radiation. Treatment includes surgical removal and chemotherapy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and D. Colon cancer (Choice A) is the second most common cause of cancer deaths in the United States. It is often detected on routine colonoscopy recommended for patients between aae 45 and 75 vears. When symptomatic. it presents with chanaes in stool caliber or frequency and iron deficiencv anemia. It would be unlikely to present witha ~ chronically increased estrogen concentrations, exogenous estrogen therapy, anovulation, obesity, and estrogen-secreting tumors. It would be less likely than ovarian cancer to cause ascites with associated nausea and vomiting. Gastric cancer (Choice C) commonly presents with weight loss, epigastric abdominal pain, nausea, and, occasionally, gastrointestinal bleeding. Areas of metastasis include the liver and lymph nodes, and, less commonly, the brain or lungs. It would not cause the pelvic mass seen in this patient. Lymphoma (Choice D) presents with weight loss, fevers, and night sweats in the setting of lymphadenopathy, with additional symptoms based on tumor location. It does not typically present with a pelvic mass.</p>", "41": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Folic acid deficiency is a common cause of anemia in pregnant patients, with other risk factors including alcohol use disorder, malabsorption, and use of folate antagonists. Laboratory studies will show an increased mean corpuscular volume with a decreased reticulocyte index and hypersegmented neutrophils. Normal methylmalonic acid concentrations will differentiate it from vitamin B42 (cobalamin) deficiency.</p><p>Folic acid deficiency is a cause of megaloblastic anemia that is common in pregnancy as a result of increased folic acid requirements, especially when accompanied by malnutrition. Other causes include alcohol use disorder, malabsorption, and the use of antifolate medications. The anemia is caused by impaired DNA synthesis in the red blood cell progenitor cells due to the absence of required cofactors. Symptoms include fatigue, pallor, lightheadedness, and shortness of breath. Laboratory studies will show a decreased hemoglobin concentration, increased mean corpuscular volume, decreased reticulocyte count, and normal methylmalonic acid concentrations. White blood cell and platelet counts are generally normal. Peripheral blood smear will show hypersegmented neutrophils. Treatment requires folic acid supplementation and red blood cell transfusion, if severe. Folic acid supplementation is important for pregnant patients, as deficiency can lead to neural tube defects in the fetus. This is most significant in the first trimester, during initial organogenesis.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, and E. Iron deficiency (Choice B) is a common cause of anemia. In pregnant patients, this is typically caused by increased metabolic demand for iron. It can also be caused by gastrointestinal bleeding, malabsorption, and malnutrition. However, it is a microcytic anemia and would present with decreased mean corpuscular volume. those of folic acid deficiency. Severe deficiencies can present with neurologic symptoms, such as dementia or subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord. However, vitamin By deficiency is less likely in pregnancy than folic acid deficiency. Sickle cell disease (Choice D) is an autosomal disorder that is caused by mutations in globin genes, which results in abnormal hemoglobin that is prone to sickling under conditions of physiologic stress (eg, low oxygen tension, dehydration, infection). This sickling leads to vascular occlusion with cyclical pain crises, functional asplenia, hemolysis, and anemia. This previously healthy patient is unlikely to have sickle cell disease. Thalassemia (Choice E) is a genetic disorder that causes ineffective erythropoiesis as a result of impaired production in alpha or beta globin proteins. It results in microcytic anemia. The severity of symptoms varies depending on whether the alpha or beta chain is affected, and how many of each respective gene are mutated. Thalassemia presents with microcytic, rather than megaloblastic, anemia.</p>", "42": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Amniotic fluid embolism classically occurs during delivery or immediately postpartum because of amniotic fluid entering the maternal circulation. It typically presents with shortness of breath, altered mental status, hypoxia, and hypotension. Complications include disseminated intravascular coagulopathy, cardiovascular collapse, and maternal and/or fetal death.</p><p>Amniotic fluid embolism is a rare and potentially fatal complication of childbirth caused by amniotic fluid entering the maternal circulation. It most commonly occurs during delivery or in the immediate postpartum period. It typically presents with acute onset of shortness of breath, altered mental status or unconsciousness, and bilateral wheezing. Tonic-clonic seizures can also occur but are less common as an initial presenting sign. Signs of fetal distress can also occur if the fetus remains in utero at the time of occurrence. Vital signs will show tachycardia, hypoxia, and severe hypotension. The patient can quickly decompensate into cardiovascular collapse and cardiopulmonary arrest. Chest x-ray may show pulmonary edema. Treatment is generally supportive, with patients often requiring fluids or blood products and mechanical ventilation. Complications include diffuse bleeding from venipuncture sites, the vagina, or a cesarean section incision as a result of disseminated intravascular ¥</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> B, C, D, E, F, and G. as Eclampsia (Choice B) presents with hypertension, proteinuria, headache, visual changes, right upper quadrant pain, and seizures in a pregnant patient after 20 weeks’ gestation, although it can also occur in the postpartum period. While tonic-clonic seizures are the hallmark of eclampsia, it would not be expected to cause the diffuse bleeding or cardiovascular collapse seen in this patient. Hepatic rupture (Choice C) is a rare complication of HELLP syndrome, which is a microangiopathic hemolysis and intravascular coagulation that occurs in a subset of pregnant patients. HELLP syndrome most commonly presents with epigastric or right upper quadrant abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, and bleeding, with further evaluation often disclosing hypertension and proteinuria. Hepatic rupture presents with abdominal pain and hemodynamic instability as a result of hemorrhage. Intravascular release of bacterial endotoxins (Choice D) classically causes septic shock, which is a term used to refer to decreased organ perfusion that occurs from overwhelming infection. It is a form of distributive shock that is classically diagnosed with fever, tachycardia, tachypnea, hypotension, and signs of infection on examination, as well as leukocytosis and lactic acidosis. The presentation is less abrupt (hours to days) compared with amniotic fluid embolism (minutes to hours). Spasm in the left anterior descending coronary artery (Choice E), or coronary artery vasospasm, classically presents as substernal or left-sided chest pain that radiates to the arm or jaw, shortness of breath, diaphoresis, and nausea. It more commonly occurs in patients who smoke cigarettes or use cocaine and is more prominent at rest. It would not explain this patient’s tonic-clonic seizure activity or diffuse bleeding. Thromboembolism in the pulmonary artery (Choice F) presents with shortness of breath, tachycardia, and hypoxia, as well as hypotension if the pulmonary embolism is massive and hemoptysis if infarction has occurred. Massive pulmonary emboli can cause cardiac arrest if the obstruction is severe. However, given this patient's coagulopathy and generalized tonic-clonic seizure during labor, amniotic fluid embolism is the most likely diagnosis. Thrombosis of cerebral veins (Choice G) has a varied clinical presentation, which includes headache, focal neurologic deficits, seizures, and encephalopathy. Small cerebral ischemic insults can also occur as a result of venous blood congestion. While pregnancy is a risk factor for cerebral vein thrombosis, it would not explain this patient's other symptoms.</p>", "43": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Paget disease of the breast refers to unilateral eczema-like patches of the nipple and areola that may also present with pruritus, bloody discharge, and nipple retraction. It is associated with underlying ductal carcinoma in situ or invasive carcinoma of the breast. Diagnosis is made by tissue biopsy.</p><p>Paget disease of the breast refers to unilateral eczema-like patches of the nipple and areola that are associated with underlying ductal carcinoma in situ or invasive carcinoma of the breast. It is widely accepted that Paget disease represents an external manifestation of underlying malignancy, though it is a much less common neanantatinn Af heraant nananr than a nalnahin bheannt mann ar ahnarmal findinna an anv anrannina maAdalith Oumntama maw alan inanlida nriuvitiuin ar a blancs: dinnhkaran an v particularly when the relatives are premenopausal at diagnosis, greatly increases the risk. Diagnosis of Paget disease and/or any underlying malignancy is confirmed with ~« biopsy, followed by any appropriate next steps if an underlying malignancy is found. Treatment depends on the presence of malignancy and its stage, but may include surgical resection, chemotherapy, and radiation therapy.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. MRI of the left breast (Choice A) may be beneficial in determining if there is an underlying malignancy. While mammography is more commonly used to evaluate patients who present with Paget disease of the breast, MRI is a more sensitive imaging modality. However, MRI would not clearly diagnose this presentation as Paget disease of the breast, making it a less appropriate next step in management than tissue biopsy. Recommendation for use of unscented soap (Choice B) is incorrect. While this patient has eczematous changes of the breast, these changes are unilateral and confined to the breast, making contact dermatitis from soap use unlikely. Additionally, these changes have remained despite a recent change in soap. These persistent abnormalities should be further characterized by tissue biopsy. Topical antifungal therapy (Choice D) may be helpful in the treatment of intertrigo, or inflammation of the inframammary folds commonly caused by Candida species. Intertrigo may occur in other areas of the body, caused by persistent heat, moisture, and friction. This patient has self-treated with powder to limit friction and moisture, with no improvement in her symptoms. Additionally, intertrigo frequently presents with macerated, erythematous skin, rather than eczematous changes, making it less likely than Paget disease of the breast. Topical corticosteroid therapy (Choice E) is useful in the treatment of eczema. Eczema is an autoimmune dermatitis that presents with pruritic, scaly patches that commonly occur on the flexor surfaces of extremities. It would be less likely to occur unilaterally on this patient’s breast than Paget disease.</p>", "44": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Pelvic organ prolapse (POP) occurs secondary to pelvic floor dysfunction and weakness, which results in the herniation of abdominopelvic organs through the vaginal wall. Complete procidentia is a severe form of POP that includes herniation of the entire anterior, posterior, and apical vaginal compartments through the introitus, such that that the patient has a degree of cystocele (bladder prolapse), rectocele (rectal prolapse), and enterocele (intestinal prolapse) caused by the descent of the pelvic organs. All or a portion of the cervix and uterus may be visible externally at the introitus in complete procidentia.</p><p>Pelvic organ prolapse (POP) occurs secondary to pelvic floor dysfunction and weakness, resulting in the herniation of abdominopelvic organs, which cause mass effect and protrusion along the vaginal wall. Complete procidentia is a severe form of POP that includes herniation of the entire anterior, posterior, and apical vaginal compartments through the introitus, such that that the patient has a degree of cystocele (bladder prolapse), rectocele (rectal prolapse), and enterocele (intestinal prolapse) caused by the descent of the pelvic organs. Additionally, all or a portion of the cervix and uterus may be visible externally at the introitus in complete procidentia. POP is more common in individuals who have had multiple childbirths, advancing age, and obesity, all of which are risk factors for pelvic floor dysfunction. These pelvic organ herniations can result in urinary, defecatory, and sexual dysfunction. Depending on the extent of herniation, other symptoms can include an external mass that may show ulceration and bleeding, as found in this patient. Diagnosis is made with a complete physical examination. Treatment includes pelvic floor exercises, vaginal pessaries, and surgical correction.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E. Bartholin duct cyst (Choice A) is caused by an obstruction of a Bartholin gland, one of which is located on each side of the introitus and provides secretions that lubricate the vagina. It presents with a small area of painful, fluctuant swelling over the posterior aspect of the labium majus. With ongoing obstruction and inflammation, an infection and Bartholin abscess can develop. It would not be expected to cause perineal bleeding or such a larae mass at the introitus. making complete procidentia more likelv. a Vaginal myoma (Choice D) is a benign tumor of the vaginal wall that may present with a palpable mass and vaginal bleeding, as found in this patient. However, these tumors are extraordinarily rare, and a complete procidentia would be significantly more likely in this patient. Vulvar carcinoma (Choice E) most commonly presents with a vulvar lesion on the labia majora that often is pruritic or friable, leading to vulvar bleeding and pain. Risk factors include tobacco use, human papillomavirus infection, and an immunocompromised status. This patient has a large mass protruding from her vagina, rather than a plaque or ulcer on her labia majora, making vulvar carcinoma less likely.</p>", "45": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Congenital rubella presents with the classic triad of cataracts, sensorineural deafness, and congenital heart disease, most often patent ductus arteriosus (PDA). PDA results from failure of the normal, extracardiac embryonic shunt between the pulmonary artery and aorta to appropriately close in the neonatal period. It results in an extracardiac left-to-right shunt and classically presents with a continuous machine-like murmur.</p><p>Congenital rubella presents with the classic triad of cataracts, sensorineural deafness, and congenital heart disease. Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA) and pulmonary artery stenosis are the most common cardiac defects. The ductus arteriosus (DA) is one of several physiologic shunts existing during the prenatal period. In utero, oxygenated blood is obtained from the placenta, not from the lungs, and the DA allows the shunting of oxygenated blood from the pulmonary artery directly to the aorta while bypassing the fetal lungs, which are filled with amniotic fluid. At birth, closure of the DA occurs by the release of prostaglandins and increased arterial oxygen tension. With increased blood oxygenation and decreased pulmonary vascular resistance following birth or with indomethacin use, prostaglandin production falls and the ductus closes. PDA presents with a heart murmur described as a continuous “machine-like” murmur best heard in the left second intercostal and left infraclavicular regions. Since blood flow through the PDA occurs in both systole and diastole, the sound will be present throughout the cardiac cycle. Certain congenital heart diseases require the presence of a shunt to allow arteriovenous admixture of oxygenated blood, for example, transposition of the great vessels. In the absence of such a requirement for shunting, a PDA results in a chronic extracardiac left-to-right shunt that may result in pulmonary hypertension or right-sided heart failure, with the risk for Eisenmenger syndrome if uncorrected. PDAs that are small often go undetected until adulthood because the left-to-right shunt is not significant enough to cause symptoms.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, and E. heart diseases, such as tetralogy of Fallot, transposition of the great arteries, and truncus arteriosus. While congenital rubella can lead to coronary artery stenosis later in life, it is not associated with congenital anomalous development of these vessels. Congenital hypertension (Choice B) causes include coarctation of the aorta, neonatal hyperthyroidism, congenital adrenal hyperplasia, and congenital anomalies of the urinary tract. While rubella can cause pulmonary hypertension if a PDA is present and not corrected, it is not associated with congenital systemic hypertension. Cyanotic heart disease (Choice C) describes congenital heart defects that result in flow of deoxygenated blood from the pulmonary to systemic circulation (eg, a right-to-left shunt). Common culprit lesions include truncus arteriosus, transposition of the great vessels, tricuspid atresia, tetralogy of Fallot, and total anomalous pulmonary venous return. Left-to-right shunting (acyanotic heart disease) occurs with ventricular septal defects, atrial septal defects, and PDA. Pulmonary artery dilation (Choice E) can develop later on in children with congenital rubella if an uncorrected PDA leads to a left-to-right shunt and pulmonary hypertension. However, this would not be present in the neonatal period. Rubella is associated with congenital pulmonary artery stenosis, not dilation.</p>", "46": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Endometrial cancer can present with postmenopausal vaginal bleeding or abnormal uterine bleeding in premenopausal women. Risk factors for its development include prolonged, unopposed exposure to estrogen, such as early menarche, late menopause, chronic anovulatory cycles, exogenous estrogen therapy, and nulliparity. Diagnosis is made with endometrial biopsy via an office procedure. If a patient cannot tolerate the procedure or if the procedure sampling is nondiagnostic, a dilatation and curettage or a hysteroscopy can be performed.</p><p>Abnormal uterine bleeding should be suspected when bleeding is irregular, unpredictable, or not associated with usual premenstrual signs and symptoms (eg, bloating, breast fullness, uterine cramps). It may be caused by malignancy, structural abnormalities (eg, cervical or endometrial polyps), adenomyosis, endometrial hyperplasia, inflammation or infection of the genital tract, coagulopathy, ovulatory dysfunction, and endocrine dysfunction (eg, thyroid disorders, prolactin, androgens), and may be iatrogenic or idiopathic. In patients over age 45 years who are perimenopausal or postmenopausal, there is a higher risk for endometrial hyperplasia and malignancy. Endometrial cancer commonly presents with postmenopausal vaginal bleeding, as in this patient. Risk factors include prolonged, unopposed exposure to estrogen, such as early menarche, late menopause, chronic anovulatory cycles (eg, polycystic ovary syndrome), exogenous estrogen therapy, nulliparity, and obesity. The most appropriate next step in management is an endometrial biopsy to evaluate for the presence of endometrial cancer. If a patient cannot tolerate the procedure or if the procedure sampling is nondiagnostic, a dilatation and curettage or a hysteroscopy can be performed.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, E, and F. v Combination oral contraceptive therapy (Choice A) is an effective method of contraception that may also be used to treat abnormal uterine bleeding secondary to anovulation, such as that seen in polycystic ovary syndrome. Oral contraceptives may also be of benefit in the treatment of primary dysmenorrhea and menorrhagia. However, this patient is perimenopausal and has had prolonged uterine bleeding, which should prompt an evaluation for endometrial cancer. Dilatation and curettage (Choice B) is useful in treating heavy or abnormal uterine bleeding that does not respond to less invasive measures. While it can be used in the diagnosis of endometrial cancer, it typically is reserved for patients who are unable to tolerate an endometrial biopsy or for those in which biopsy was nondiagnostic, as it is a more invasive surgical procedure requiring anesthesia. Hysteroscopy (Choice D) is frequently used to evaluate the uterus for structural causes of irregular menstruation, as it can visualize the entire endometrial cavity. This makes it useful in the diagnosis of polyps and fibroids. Hysteroscopy can also be used to obtain an endometrial biopsy but is a more invasive option. Progestin-only oral contraceptive therapy (Choice E) would not be the most appropriate next step in management of this case. While high concentrations of progesterone can decrease vaginal bleeding, it would be inappropriate to try to minimize this patient's bleeding without first establishing a diagnosis. Reassurance and follow-up examination in 6 months (Choice F) is incorrect. This patient has had heavy vaginal bleeding for 3 months, which has likely significantly affected her life. Additionally, her symptoms are concerning for endometrial cancer, and she should undergo further evaluation to confirm or refute this diagnosis. Delaying diagnosis would increase the morbidity and mortality associated with a possible underlying malignancy.</p>", "47": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Factor V Leiden is an autosomal dominant condition caused by a point mutation in the F5 gene of factor V. Affected women are at increased risk for deep venous thrombosis as well as uteroplacental arterial thrombosis, which can lead to placental infarction and fetal demise. Diagnosis can be confirmed with genetic testing or a functional assay evaluating activated protein C resistance.</p><p>Factor V Leiden is an autosomal dominant condition wherein coagulation factor V is resistant to inactivation by protein C, leading to a hypercoagulable state. Caused by a point mutation in the F5 gene of factor V, it is the most common inherited cause of hypercoagulability in white patients. Pregnancy causes a hypercoagulable state due to the overexpression of coagulation factors |, II, VII, VIII, IX, and X, the underexpression of anticoagulant protein S, and venous stasis in the pelvis and lower extremities caused by hormonal changes and compression by the gravid uterus. Thromboembolic events during pregnancy or the postpartum period are therefore a common presentation of Factor V Leiden, as these patients are already predisposed to such events. Other presentations include thromboembolic events within the uteroplacental arteries, leading to placental infarction and possible intrauterine fetal demise, and cerebral vein thrombosis. A heritable coagulation disorder should be suspected in this patient with a history of provoked deep venous thrombosis (DVT) in combination with an extensive family history of thromboembolic events. Diagnosis can be confirmed with genetic testing or a functional assay evaluating activated protein C resistance. Venous thromboembolic event prophylaxis with low-molecular-weight heparin (eg, enoxaparin) is indicated for gravid patients with Factor V Leiden and personal history of thrombosis/thromboembolism. Warfarin is a teratogen and should be avoided. Novel oral anticoagulants such as rivaroxaban and apixaban are not recommended because of fetal safety concerns.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A RB C and F a as disseminated intravascular coagulopathy and normal in common clotting factor deficiencies such as hemophilia A or B. Fibrinogen is among the clotting factors overexpressed in pregnancy, leading to a hypercoagulable state, but acquired hyperfibrinogenemia would not explain this patient's extensive family history of venous thromboembolism (VTE). Measurement of prothrombin and partial thromboplastin times (Choice B) is used to evaluate function of the extrinsic and intrinsic coagulation pathways, respectively, in patients with coagulopathy. Partial thromboplastin time may be decreased or normal in Factor V Leiden; genetic or functional testing are more sensitive and specific in the diagnosis of this disease. Measurement of serum factor VIII (antihemophilic factor) antigen concentration (Choice C) may be performed for the diagnosis of hemophilia A, an X-linked coagulopathy caused by a deficiency in factor VIII synthesis. Factor VIII is also often decreased in von Willebrand disease, a hereditary bleeding disorder, as von Willebrand factor binds and stabilizes it in the plasma. This patient's history is suspicious for an inherited thrombophilia, not coagulopathy. Acquired increased factor VIII concentration is found in conditions including pregnancy, malignancy, and hyperthyroidism, but the association with VTE is weak. Repeat venous Doppler ultrasonography (Choice E) would be indicated if this patient presented with features of DVT, such as unilateral lower extremity pain, erythema, and edema, along with tenderness on palpation and pain exacerbated with passive stretch of the foot. This patient is asymptomatic and requires genetic testing for suspected inherited thrombophilia in the setting of her significant family history and current pregnancy.</p>", "48": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Gestational diabetes can lead to an increased risk for congenital anomalies, spontaneous abortion and stillbirth, macrosomia, shoulder dystocia, and birth trauma, along with neonatal hypoglycemia, hyperbilirubinemia, polycythemia, and an increased risk for neonatal respiratory distress syndrome. Screening is typically performed with a 1-hour maternal glucose tolerance test between 24 and 28 weeks of pregnancy, though it can be performed earlier for patients with suspected preexisting diabetes or for those with risk factors for diabetes.</p><p>A 1-hour maternal glucose tolerance test is typically performed between 24 and 28 weeks of pregnancy to screen for gestational diabetes. However, this test can be done at the first prenatal visit if there is suspicion of underlying or preexisting diabetes or in patients who have a BMI greater than or equal to 25 kg/m? in addition to another risk factor, such as first-degree relatives with diabetes mellitus, hypertension, previous macrosomia or gestational diabetes, or a history of impaired glucose tolerance. Gestational or preexisting diabetes mellitus can lead to an increased risk for congenital anomalies, spontaneous abortion and stillbirth, macrosomia, shoulder dystocia, and birth trauma. Neonatal effects of diabetes can include hypoglycemia caused by a sudden change in the maternal-fetal glucose balance, hyperbilirubinemia, polycythemia, and an increased risk for neonatal respiratory distress syndrome. Therefore, the early recognition of both gestational diabetes and preexisting diabetes mellitus allows for the initiation of appropriate treatment, decreased average blood glucose concentrations, and improved neonatal outcomes.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, C, D, and E. o Rh(D) alloimmunization when exposed to Rh(D) antigens from the fetal circulation. Repeat antibody screening is not indicated for this Rh(D)-positive patient with uncomplicated pregnancy. Repeat testing for N. gonorrhoeae and C. trachomatis (Choice C) is not indicated for this asymptomatic patient with no high-risk sexual behavior. Repeat testing for previously negative patients may be considered if symptoms of cervicitis develop (eg, foul-smelling or discolored discharge, pruritus, dysuria) or for patients who have subsequently engaged in high-risk sexual behavior. Repeat urine culture (Choice D) following a negative prenatal urine culture can be considered for patients with symptoms of urinary tract infection (UTI) and in those at high tisk for UTI, such as those with recurrent UTIs, urinary tract anomalies, or diabetes mellitus. It is not indicated for this asymptomatic patient with a negative routine urine culture screen. Repeat ultrasonography (Choice E) is not yet indicated for this patient in her second trimester with a normal 20-week ultrasound (routinely performed for anatomic survey and growth monitoring). Further, routine ultrasonography is not recommended until the patient reaches 28 weeks’ gestation or later; these scans are used to monitor fetal growth and amniotic fluid index.</p>", "49": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Annual screening for Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae is recommended for all sexually active patients under age 25 years. Chlamydia typically presents with urethral or vaginal discharge, dysuria, urethritis, and dyspareunia. Gonorrhea commonly causes mucopurulent discharge and pruritus. However, both infections may also remain asymptomatic. Chlamydia is treated with azithromycin or doxycycline. Gonorrhea is treated with ceftriaxone.</p><p>Testing for Chlamydia trachomatis and Neisseria gonorrhoeae is recommended by the United States Preventive Services Task Force for all sexually active patients under age 25 years. C. trachomatis is a sexually transmitted infection that classically presents with urethritis, dysuria, dyspareunia, and urethral or vaginal discharge. N. gonorrhoeae is a sexually transmitted infection that presents most commonly with mucopurulent discharge and pruritus. However, both infections can also be asymptomatic, thus the recommendation for screening. Complications include cervicitis, pelvic inflammatory disease, tubo-ovarian abscess, and long-term issues with fertility. Diagnosis is made with a nucleic acid amplification test. Treatment consists of either azithromycin or doxycycline for chlamydia and ceftriaxone for gonorrhea.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, C, D, and E. Hepatitis B surface antigen and hepatitis B surface antibody assays (Choice A) are useful in the diagnosis of active hepatitis B infection, which is a blood-borne infection. Acute hepatitis B presents with fever, abdominal pain, nausea, vomiting, anorexia, and jaundice. While hepatitis B surface antibodies are present in those vaccinated ¥ for infection is not recommended for patients who are vaccinated, such as this patient. Human papillomavirus nucleic acid testing (Choice B) is used to detect the presence of human papillomavirus infection, which is a sexually transmitted viral infection that can cause cervical/vulvar and penile/anal carcinoma, as well as condylomata acuminata. Screening for this virus is often not recommended until a patient is 30 years old, because many infections occurring in younger patients will be self-limited. Pap smear (Choice C) refers to an evaluation of cells of the cervix as a screening modality for cervical cancer. It is recommended to start for patients at age 21 years and be repeated every 3 years. After age 30 years, it can be repeated every 5 years if it is done in conjunction with human papillomavirus co-testing and both results are normal. This patient is 15 years old, and thus Pap smear is not yet recommended. Rapid plasma reagin (Choice D) is the serologic test for syphilis and is not recommended in asymptomatic patients. Syphilis has a multitude of presentations depending on its stage. Primary syphilis can present with a genital lesion that is nonpainful, while secondary syphilis presents with a maculopapular rash and condyloma lata. Tertiary syphilis, the most advanced form, has symptoms of neurosyphilis (eg, tabes dorsalis, Argyll Robertson pupil, ataxia) and aortitis. This patient is not experiencing any of these symptoms. Repeat hepatitis B vaccine series (Choice E) is not necessary. This patient is up-to-date on her vaccines, and unless she demonstrates nonimmunity to hepatitis B, repeat vaccination is not required.</p>", "50": "<p class=\"exp-obj\">Educational Objective: Lochia is normal vaginal discharge following delivery caused by the shedding of the decidua. Lochia rubra, the first stage, contains blood and lasts for up to 10 days. This progresses to a serous discharge, lochia serosa, and finally a white to yellow discharge, lochia alba.</p><p>Vaginal discharge following delivery is termed lochia and may last during the period of the puerperium for 4 to 6 weeks. It is caused by the shedding of the superficial decidua and progresses through three stages. The early discharge, lochia rubra, contains blood and may last for up to 10 days. Lochia slowly becomes less bloody and more serous, lochia serosa, for approximately 2 to 3 weeks before finally becoming white to yellow in color, lochia alba. Physical examination, laboratory studies, and diagnostic imaging are not typically performed but would be normal as lochia is a physiologic, rather than pathologic, condition.</p><p><b>Incorrect Answers:</b> A, B, D, and E. Bacterial vaginitis (Choice A) is caused by vaginal overgrowth of Gardnerella vaginalis. \\t presents with gray, thin, malodorous vaginal discharge, a vaginal pH greater than 4.5, and a fish-like odor on KOH testing. Identification of clue cells on wet mount microscopy is a useful confirmatory test. This patient has thick, white vaginal discharge, making lochia alba more likely. Candidiasis (Choice B) is caused by overgrowth of normal vaginal Candida flora, most commonly Candida albicans. It presents with vaginal pruritus, vulvar erythema, and a thick, white discharge with normal vaginal pH and negative KOH testing. Risk factors include diabetes mellitus and recent antibiotic or corticosteroid use. While this patient has white vaginal discharge, she has no other symptoms or risk factors to suggest candidiasis. Retained products of conception (Choice D) can lead to secondary postpartum hemorrhage, which is bleeding that occurs 24 hours to 12 weeks after delivery. In addition to bleeding, retained products of conception can become infected, which presents with abdominal pain, fever, and malodorous vaginal discharge. This patient has no signs of bleeding or infection, making retained products of conception unlikely. Undiagnosed vaginal laceration (Choice E) is not the cause of this patient's discharge. While most vaginal lacerations are diagnosed immediately following birth because of ongoing bleeding, an undiagnosed vaginal laceration may present with delayed vaginal bleeding. However, it would be unlikely to cause the white discharge this patient is experiencing.</p>"};
